// Surah Yusuf, for learners aged 16 and over.
// Based on "Lessons from Surah Yusuf" by Dr Yasir Qadhi (Kube Publishing), summarised in our own words.
// The whole book: Parts 1-13 and the conclusion (lessons 1-32). Same shape as LESSONS / FINAL in learn/index.html.
// Lives under functions/ so it is never served as a static file: only /api/content hands it out.

import { ayat, SOURCE } from "./yusuf-verses.js";

const LESSONS = [
/* ---------- Part 1: The Family of Ya‘qub ---------- */
{"id":1,"verses":[1,3],"title":"Alif-Lam-Ra: The Clear Book","short":"Verses 1–3: the Book and the best of stories","big":"The surah opens with disjointed letters, praises the Qur’an as a clear Book sent down in Arabic so that we may understand, and announces “the best of stories”: a story the Prophet ﷺ could only have known through revelation.","remember":["Huruf al-muqatta‘at: their true meaning is unknown, but they are almost always followed by praise of the Qur’an.","Tilka (“those”) points to something distant: a sign of the Qur’an’s exalted rank, even though it is in our hands.","Kitab mubin: clear in its message to anyone sincere, and clear in its source, which is Allah.","Anzala: sent down all at once on Laylat al-Qadr. Nazzala: brought down in parts over twenty-three years.","Only the Arabic text is Allah’s revelation; a translation is not Allah’s Book.","Qissah comes from following footprints: to hear a story is to walk in someone’s footsteps."],"more":[{"t":"The disjointed letters","l":["They are called “broken letters” because they do not form words. Some surahs open with one (Qaf), some with two (Ya Sin), some with more.","Scholars give more than fifteen opinions on why they were revealed; their true meaning is known to Allah.","They use fourteen letters, exactly half the Arabic alphabet. One view links them to the Verses of Challenge (17:88, 11:13, 10:38): the Qur’an is made of Quraysh’s own letters, yet they could not produce anything like it."]},{"t":"“Those are the verses of the clear Book”","l":["Allah Himself divided the Qur’an into ayat and surahs.","Ayah also means a sign or a miracle. Allah uses the same word for creation and for verses: every verse carries a message, and every verse is a miracle.","Kitab is the revelation in written form; Qur’an is its recited form. It was preserved through both at once.","Mubin: anyone can draw basic guidance from it, even from a translation, though deeper interpretation needs knowledge. And its miraculous nature makes plain that it is from Allah (15:9)."]},{"t":"“We sent it down as an Arabic Qur’an”","l":["“We” is the plural of majesty. Ibn Taymiyyah held that it means Allah and His angels, as with rain, wind and revelation, which involve angels. When Allah speaks of worship He always uses the singular: “I am Allah… so worship Me” (20:14).","The Qur’an is kalamullah. Allah recited it in Arabic to Jibril, and the chain of recitation runs unbroken: Allah, Jibril, the Prophet ﷺ, the Companions, us. This is why we feel its divine nature even without understanding the words.","Arabic was chosen to make understanding easier, which should move us to learn it."]},{"t":"Why open with revelation?","l":["To remind the Prophet ﷺ of Allah’s favours and console him in hardship, a common motif (93:3, and al-Fath, al-Ahzab, al-Qalam, al-Inshirah).","The greatest favour is the Qur’an itself, and the Ummah shares in it.","The author also counts the descent of the Qur’an among the proofs that Allah is above His creation, in a way that befits Him."]},{"t":"The best of stories","l":["Stories grab attention and teach memorable lessons. They show faith put into action, they are ‘ibrah (lesson and warning), and they strengthen the heart (11:120).","“Best of stories” has two meanings: every Qur’anic story is the best of its kind, true and told with perfect wisdom; and among them, Yusuf’s is singled out as the best.","Its core lesson: evil never ultimately succeeds; righteousness prevails.","“You were among the unaware (ghafilin)” is not criticism. The Prophet ﷺ had no access to this story in Makkah, so it is a favour to him and a proof of prophethood."]}],"home":"Read verses 1–3 in Arabic and in translation. Note one thing the Arabic carries that the translation cannot, and one reason it should motivate you to learn Arabic.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 1–3","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Huruf al-muqatta‘at","The disjointed letters"],["Tilka","“Those”: a pointer to something exalted"],["Kitab mubin","The clear Book"],["Anzala","Sent down all at once"],["Nazzala","Sent down in parts"],["Qissah","A story; from following footprints"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"How many letters are used in the disjointed letters?","o":["Seven","Fourteen, half the alphabet","All twenty-eight"],"a":1,"w":"Fourteen letters, exactly half the Arabic alphabet."},{"q":"Why does the verse use tilka (“those”), a word for something distant?","o":["The Qur’an had not yet been revealed","To show the Qur’an’s exalted rank","Because the verse refers to earlier scriptures"],"a":1,"w":"The distant pointer conveys its exalted, transcendent status."},{"q":"What are the two senses of “mubin”?","o":["Clear in message and clear in source","Long and short","Written and recited"],"a":0,"w":"Its guidance is clear to the sincere, and it is clearly from Allah."},{"q":"According to Ibn Taymiyyah, “We” in such verses means…","o":["The prophets","Allah and His angels","The believers"],"a":1,"w":"Allah and His angels; worship is always commanded in the singular."},{"q":"What is the difference between anzala and nazzala?","o":["Makkah versus Madinah","All at once versus in parts","Written versus spoken"],"a":1,"w":"Anzala is the single descent on Laylat al-Qadr; nazzala is the gradual one over 23 years."},{"q":"What does “you were among the unaware” show?","o":["A rebuke of the Prophet ﷺ","That the story was well known in Makkah","A favour, and a proof of prophethood"],"a":2,"w":"He could not have known the story except through revelation."}]},
{"id":2,"verses":[4,7],"title":"The Dream and a Father’s Counsel","short":"Verses 4–7: the dream, envy and Allah’s choice","big":"Young Yusuf dreams that eleven stars, the sun and the moon prostrate to him. Ya‘qub, a prophet and a loving father, tells him to keep it from his brothers, warns him about Shaytan, and tells him how Allah will choose him, as He chose his fathers Ibrahim and Ishaq.","remember":["Four generations of prophets: Yusuf, son of Ya‘qub, son of Ishaq, son of Ibrahim.","Three kinds of dreams: from Allah, from Shaytan, and from our own thoughts. A prophet’s dream is wahy.","Ya‘qub’s first instinct was protection: he knew his sons and kept peace in the family.","Envy devours good deeds as fire devours wood, and unlike most feelings, harbouring it makes us liable.","Blame Shaytan when criticising others, to leave the door to repentance open; never when criticising yourself.","Verse 7 is the only place where the signs are for “those who ask”."],"more":[{"t":"The family of Ibrahim","l":["The Prophet ﷺ called Yusuf “the noble, son of the noble, son of the noble, son of the noble” (al-Adab al-Mufrad 896).","Isma‘il, father of the Arabs, produced one prophet: Muhammad ﷺ, the answer to Ibrahim’s du‘a (2:128). Ishaq, through Ya‘qub (Isra’il), produced a chain of prophets ending with ‘Isa.","Biblical material may be used as extra reference, never for creed or rulings: “neither believe nor deny” (Bukhari 3461). In that account Yusuf and Binyamin were full brothers, sons of Rahil; the majority view, including Ibn ‘Abbas, is that she died giving birth to Binyamin.","Most scholars say Yusuf was about seven years old at the time of the dream."]},{"t":"The dream and its symbols","l":["Idh (“remember when”) is the standard Qur’anic opening for a story.","After the Prophet ﷺ, only al-mubashshirat, true good dreams, remain of prophethood (Bukhari 6990).","The stars, sun and moon are “shining people”: eleven brothers and two parents. Some say the sun is the mother, whose right is three times the father’s; others say the sun is Ya‘qub.","The meaning: Yusuf would rise above his whole family, including his prophet father."]},{"t":"“Do not relate your vision to your brothers”","l":["The Arabic has double emphasis, “they will plot against you a severe plot”, which translations lose.","“A dream is like a bird flying; when it is interpreted, it lands. Narrate it only to someone who cares or an interpreter” (Tirmidhi 2278).","A parent’s love is selfless: the dream meant the son would surpass the father, yet Ya‘qub felt no resentment.","Do not flaunt blessings, because it invites envy."]},{"t":"Envy, and blaming Shaytan","l":["Hasad is a burning desire for what others have, seeing them as undeserving. It contradicts iman, as if saying: “O Allah, why did You give them this?”","“Beware of envy, for it devours good deeds as fire devours wood” (Abu Dawud 4903).","Ya‘qub foresaw the plot, yet he blamed Shaytan, never calling his sons evil.","Shaytan has no authority over us; he only whispers. On Judgement Day he will say: “Do not blame me; blame yourselves” (14:22)."]},{"t":"Allah’s choice, and lessons for those who ask","l":["Ijtiba’ means to sift through and pick the best. Yusuf is mentioned apart from Ya‘qub’s family: his blessings equalled theirs combined.","Al-Asbat, Yusuf’s brothers, later became prophets (2:136), but the majority say they were not yet prophets when they committed the crime.","Allah protects the children of the righteous: Khidr rebuilt the wall “because their father was righteous” (18:82). The best way to secure righteous children is to be pious ourselves.","Al-‘Alim, al-Hakim: I know what I am doing; I saved your fathers from the knife and the fire; I will test you as I tested them, and you will emerge victorious.","The surah points to Allah’s power and Sunnah, the truth of the Messenger ﷺ, and the morals of the story. It was revealed to console him: like Yusuf, he would triumph."]}],"home":"This week, when you see a blessing someone else has, say “ma sha’ Allah” and make du‘a for them, the opposite of envy. Then ask yourself one question about the surah, since its signs are for “those who ask”.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 4–7","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Idh","“Remember when”: the opening of a story"],["Al-mubashshirat","True good dreams"],["Hasad","Envy"],["Ijtiba’","To sift through and choose the best"],["Al-Asbat","The sons of Ya‘qub"],["Al-Hakim","The Wise, whose judgement is perfect"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"About how old was Yusuf at the time of the dream, according to most scholars?","o":["Seven","Seventeen","Thirty"],"a":0,"w":"Most scholars say about seven."},{"q":"What did the sun and the moon represent in the dream?","o":["Two kings of Egypt","Yusuf’s parents","Yusuf’s two closest brothers"],"a":1,"w":"The eleven stars were his brothers; the sun and moon his parents."},{"q":"What is the etiquette of blaming Shaytan?","o":["Blame him for others’ faults, never for your own","Blame him for your own faults, never for others’","Never mention him"],"a":0,"w":"It leaves others room to repent, and leaves us no excuse."},{"q":"Why is envy different from most feelings?","o":["It is always harmless","Harbouring it makes us liable, even before acting","It only harms the envied"],"a":1,"w":"Jealousy is the exception: holding on to it makes us accountable."},{"q":"What does ijtiba’ literally mean?","o":["To forgive","To sift through and pick the best","To travel far away"],"a":1,"w":"Allah chose Yusuf, picking the best."},{"q":"In verse 7, the signs are for…","o":["Those who think","Those who ask","Those who travel"],"a":1,"w":"The only place in the Qur’an where the signs are for those who ask."}]},
{"id":3,"verses":[8,14],"title":"Envy Becomes a Plot","short":"Verses 8–14: the brothers’ plan","big":"The ten older brothers resent their father’s love for Yusuf and Binyamin. Envy clouds their reasoning: they plan to kill or exile Yusuf, settle on the well, and use emotional pressure to make Ya‘qub let him go, against his own intuition.","remember":["They accused Ya‘qub only of loving the two more, not of giving them more.","Parents must be equal in gifts; love is not in our control and is not held to account.","“Sin now, repent later” is a classic trick of Shaytan. One sin leads to a lie, then to an oath on that lie.","When forced between two haram options, choose the lesser evil.","Firasah: Ya‘qub’s heart was not convinced, despite their persuasion.","The wolf was likely a tawriyah: a true statement hiding the real reason, “I do not want him to go”."],"more":[{"t":"“Our father is clearly mistaken”","l":["The extra lam on Yusuf’s name adds emphasis: “this is an undeniable fact”. “His brother” is Binyamin, by consensus, still a newborn.","‘Usbah means a large group: the ten leaned on their numbers, hinting they were more useful to their father.","They meant mistaken in his preference, not in his faith.","A father naturally loves his more righteous children more, which is the root of their jealousy, and shows they were less righteous than Yusuf."]},{"t":"Fairness between children","l":["Al-Nu‘man’s father wanted the Prophet ﷺ to witness a gift to one son. He asked: “Have you given all your children the same?… Would you not like them all to honour you equally?” (Ibn Majah 2375).","Gifts must be equal. Love cannot be commanded, but a parent can act to prevent rivalry, as Ya‘qub did."]},{"t":"They lost both goals","l":["Envy impaired their reasoning: removing the beloved does not redirect love. Absence makes the heart grow fonder.","Spiritually, “one sin, then righteousness” became a chain of sins. In this world, they broke their father’s heart instead of winning it.","Never use ignoble means for a noble goal: wanting a father’s love is halal; their method was haram and backfired.","One positive: intending to repent is better than sinning without remorse, and Allah did forgive them in the end. It is not an excuse, but it is better.","Most scholars hold they were adults: adults also play, and they later sought forgiveness (12:97), which children are not accountable for."]},{"t":"The well, and the lesser evil","l":["One brother, possibly the eldest, who appears again later, minimised the harm: Yusuf might become a slave, but he would live.","Their crimes were many: cutting ties of kinship, separating a child from his father, violating their father’s rights, all magnified because Ya‘qub was a prophet.","Jubb is a primitive unwalled well; bi’r is a built one. Ghayabah, the dark depth, comes from ghayb. Yaltaqithu, “pick him up”, suggests something found by chance. The precise wording shows they knew exactly how wicked the plan was.","“Envy and hatred are the shaver: it shaves faith, not hair” (Tirmidhi 2434). The cure: love one another and spread salam (Muslim 54)."]},{"t":"Persuading their father","l":["Nasihun, “well-wishers”, shares a root with nasihah: wanting good for someone. The wording implies Ya‘qub had never let Yusuf go with them before.","La‘ib, play, is allowed in moderation: the Prophet ﷺ raced ‘A’ishah, and let the Abyssinians display their spears in the mosque (Bukhari 3529). Lahw is pure time-wasting, such as gambling.","Emotional pressure: guilt (“Why don’t you trust us?”) and “Don’t you want Yusuf to be happy?”. “We will surely guard him” carries triple emphasis, the same verb Allah uses for guarding the Qur’an (15:9).","“Beware the firasah of the believer, for he sees with the light of Allah” (Tirmidhi 3127).","Ya‘qub handed them the cover story they would use. Sinners rarely think three steps ahead."]}],"home":"If you have siblings, do one thing this week that builds love between you: a gift, a kind word or a shared salam. If you are a parent or older sibling, check whether you are being fair in what you give.","game":{"type":"order","title":"From envy to action","help":"Tap the steps in the order they happen in the surah.","items":["They say their father loves Yusuf and his brother more","They propose killing Yusuf or casting him out","One brother proposes the well instead","They ask why their father does not trust them","They ask to take Yusuf out to play","Ya‘qub says he fears a wolf","They answer that they are a strong group"]},"quiz":[{"q":"What exactly did the brothers accuse their father of?","o":["Giving Yusuf more wealth","Loving Yusuf and his brother more","Neglecting his worship"],"a":1,"w":"Only of loving them more, not of giving them more."},{"q":"What does Islam require of parents between their children?","o":["Equal love in the heart","Equality in material gifts","Nothing in particular"],"a":1,"w":"Gifts must be equal; love is not in our control."},{"q":"“After that you can become righteous people” is an example of…","o":["Sincere repentance","Shaytan’s trick: sin now, repent later","Good planning"],"a":1,"w":"One sin led to a lie, and then an oath on that lie."},{"q":"What principle did the brother who suggested the well follow?","o":["Choose the lesser of two evils","Always obey the majority","Avoid all risk"],"a":0,"w":"When forced between two harms, choose the lesser."},{"q":"What is the difference between la‘ib and lahw?","o":["La‘ib is forbidden; lahw is allowed","La‘ib is light play allowed in moderation; lahw is pure time-wasting","There is no difference"],"a":1,"w":"The Prophet ﷺ raced ‘A’ishah; gambling is lahw."},{"q":"Why do most scholars say Ya‘qub mentioned the wolf?","o":["Wolves had attacked before","As a tawriyah hiding his real reason","He saw it in a dream"],"a":1,"w":"A true statement that concealed “I do not want him to go”."}]},
/* ---------- Part 2: Sold Into Slavery ---------- */
{"id":4,"verses":[15,22],"title":"The Well, Patience and Egypt","short":"Verses 15–22: from the well to a minister’s house","big":"The brothers throw Yusuf into the well, where Allah calms him with a promise. They return at night with fake tears and false blood, and Ya‘qub answers with beautiful patience. A caravan finds Yusuf, sells him cheaply, and Allah places him in the house of al-‘Aziz, where he grows into a man of wisdom and knowledge.","remember":["Allah never condemns the brothers by name or adjective: their sin appears only through their own confession, because they repented.","In the well, Allah inspired Yusuf (ilham): he would not die, he would be reunited, and he would have the upper hand.","Dam kadhib, “lying blood”: Ya‘qub replied with bal, a total rejection of their story.","Sabrun jamil: patience that seeks no sympathy from people and takes grief straight to Allah.","Wa-llahu al-musta‘an: “Allah’s help is sought”. Memorise it and use it often.","Tawakkul: do your part, as Ya‘qub did, and know the outcome is Allah’s."],"more":[{"t":"The dignity of the Qur’an","l":["“When they took him away…” is left incomplete: concise speech that skips what is not needed.","The Qur’an mentions evil briefly and with dignity, as with “relieved yourselves” and “touched your wives” (4:43). Unlike much modern media, it never describes sin in vivid detail (24:19).","True forgiveness means not reminding people of their mistakes, and not telling others."]},{"t":"The promise in the well","l":["Allah placed sakinah, tranquillity, in the frightened child’s heart, and the emphasis made the promise certain.","For the Prophet ﷺ it was a hint: his people abandoned him, yet he would triumph, as in “He will bring you back home” (28:85).","Knowing the outcome brings comfort. Our promised outcome is Jannah for the righteous, and remembering it carries us through hardship.","Types of wahy: instinct for non-humans, like the bees (16:68); revelation to prophets through an angel (42:51); and ilham, an inner inspiration, as given to Musa’s mother (28:7). The majority say Yusuf’s was ilham, since he was still a child."]},{"t":"Fake tears and false blood","l":["They came back after sunset to look as if they had been searching, weeping for effect, and turned Ya‘qub’s own fear of the wolf against him.","Shurayh al-Qadi stayed unmoved while a woman wept before him: “The brothers of Yusuf also came weeping, and they were the wrongdoers.” She was later found guilty. Judge on facts, not emotion.","Ya‘qub said “your souls have tempted you”, not “you did wrong”: he appealed to their conscience and left room for repentance."]},{"t":"Beautiful patience","l":["Sabr is restraining yourself when the pain is sharp and every urge says act out. “The strong man is the one who controls himself when angry” (Bukhari 5763).","Three types: patience in calamity, in refraining from sin, and in persisting in worship. Losing a child needs the highest level.","Later Ya‘qub says: “I complain of my anguish and sorrow only to Allah” (12:86). Allah is with the patient (2:153), and they are rewarded without measure."]},{"t":"Sold, and established in the land","l":["The water-drawer lowered his own bucket, proof of a rarely used well. They hid Yusuf as merchandise, made no attempt to find his family, and smuggled him to Egypt.","Bakhs: Yusuf was priceless, so any price was a loss; and they sold him far below his value, perhaps a tenth. What we get for free, we often fail to value, like those born Muslim who appreciate Islam less than converts.","Al-‘Aziz, likely the minister of finance, told his wife to honour his stay. Ibn Mas‘ud named him among three people of outstanding firasah, with the shepherd’s daughter in Musa’s story (28:26) and Abu Bakr choosing ‘Umar.","Nothing was chance: the brothers’ crime set off the journey from a remote well to a minister’s house. “Allah prevails in His affair, but most people do not know.”","At maturity (ashudd), most say about 19–20, Allah gave him hukm, wisdom and power, and ‘ilm, knowledge. “Thus We reward the muhsinin.”"]}],"home":"Memorise “wa-llahu al-musta‘an” and say it the next time something hurts. Choose one hardship you are facing and, instead of telling people, take it to Allah in du‘a.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Deception or patience?","help":"Is each one part of the brothers’ deception, or Ya‘qub’s response?","buckets":["The brothers’ deception","Ya‘qub’s response"],"items":[["Coming home after sunset",0],["Weeping fake tears",0],["The shirt with false blood",0],["“You will not believe us, however truthful we are”",0],["“Your souls have tempted you”",1],["Beautiful patience",1],["“Allah’s help is sought”",1],["Complaining only to Allah",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the Qur’an never use a negative adjective for the brothers?","o":["They were already prophets","Its dignity in mentioning evil, and because they repented","Their sin was small"],"a":1,"w":"Their sin appears only through their own confession."},{"q":"What three things did the inspiration in the well imply?","o":["He would be rich, famous and powerful","He would not die, he would be reunited, and he would have the upper hand","He would escape that night"],"a":1,"w":"Three promises, made certain by emphasis."},{"q":"What lesson did Shurayh al-Qadi draw from the brothers’ tears?","o":["Tears always prove guilt","Judge on facts, not emotion","Never trust a family"],"a":1,"w":"The brothers wept, and they were the wrongdoers."},{"q":"What makes patience “beautiful” (sabrun jamil)?","o":["Not seeking people’s sympathy, and taking grief to Allah","Never feeling sad","Hiding all tears forever"],"a":0,"w":"Ya‘qub complained only to Allah (12:86)."},{"q":"What does bakhs, the price Yusuf was sold for, show?","o":["He was sold far below his value","He was sold for gold","He was given away as a gift"],"a":0,"w":"So few coins that they could be counted."},{"q":"Who did Ibn Mas‘ud name as having outstanding firasah?","o":["Al-‘Aziz, the shepherd’s daughter and Abu Bakr","Ya‘qub, Yusuf and Binyamin","The king, his wife and the caravan"],"a":0,"w":"Each saw greatness at first sight."}]},
/* ---------- Part 3: The Seduction ---------- */
{"id":5,"verses":[23,24],"title":"The Trial of Temptation","short":"Verses 23–24: “Allah is my refuge”","big":"The lady of the house, in whose home Yusuf lived as a slave, repeatedly tried to seduce him, and finally locked the doors. His instant response was to turn to Allah. The certainty that Allah was watching kept him from sin, and Allah calls him one of His chosen, sincere servants.","remember":["Rawadathu implies repeated attempts that grew stronger as he matured, not a single event.","Ma‘adha-llah: “the One whose refuge I seek is Allah”. Turn to Allah instantly; we cannot beat temptation alone.","“No man is alone with a woman except that Shaytan is the third” (Tirmidhi 2165).","Feeling desire is not a sin; acting on it is. An evil intention left undone is recorded as a good deed (Muslim 235).","The burhan, by the strongest opinion, was his certainty that Allah was watching him.","Mukhlasin (chosen) and mukhlisin (sincere): sincerity is the way to be chosen."],"more":[{"t":"Half of all beauty","l":["On the Mi‘raj the Prophet ﷺ said: “I saw Yusuf, who was given half of all beauty” (Muslim 162).","Allah gave him that beauty, then tested him with it."]},{"t":"Four pressures at once","l":["Allah calls her “the lady in whose house he lived”, not “the wife of al-‘Aziz”, to show how hard this was for Yusuf.","He lived in her house, under her authority; he was a slave with no rights and she a free, elite woman; he was a young man in his prime; and he felt there was no escape.","Ghallaqat: the shaddah shows she locked and double-locked every door. She had planned the moment. Hayta lak is a rare word that conveys how crude her invitation was.","The Qur’an gives no further detail, because none is needed."]},{"t":"“Allah is my refuge”","l":["He did not say a‘udhu billah, “I seek refuge in Allah”, but ma‘adha-llah: Allah alone is the perfect protector.","Innahu rabbi: many exegetes say “my master”, al-‘Aziz, who treated him well. The author prefers “my Lord”, Allah: the verse begins and ends with Allah, and zina is wrong whatever the husband’s kindness.","“If you are tempted by Shaytan, seek refuge with Allah” (41:36)."]},{"t":"The most debated verse","l":["The majority read it as “he would have desired her, had he not seen the sign”, so he felt no desire: a prophet is too noble.","A minority, to which the author leans, take it at face value: hamm is desire without action. He felt it and controlled it, which shows his humanity and earns him more praise.","On the strongest view, prophets are protected from major sins and from anything that damages their character. They may make human mistakes, as Adam did, but never act on indecent thoughts, and any slip is corrected at once.","Al-su’ is any evil; al-fahsha’ is sexual evil. Naming both clears Yusuf completely."]},{"t":"Chosen, and sincere","l":["Both readings are authentic revelation; most Muslims recite the riwayah of Hafs ‘an ‘Asim.","Mukhlasin: chosen, purified servants. Mukhlisin: sincere servants. If we are sincere, Allah will turn evil away from us.","Among the seven shaded by Allah is “a man called by a woman of rank and beauty who says, ‘I fear Allah’” (Bukhari 620). Yusuf is the model of this."]}],"home":"Memorise “ma‘adha-llah” and 41:36. The next time you are tempted, say it straight away, and remind yourself that Allah is watching.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 23–24","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Rawadathu","She tried to seduce him, again and again"],["Ghallaqat al-abwab","She locked and double-locked the doors"],["Ma‘adha-llah","The One whose refuge I seek is Allah"],["Hamm","Desire without action"],["Burhan","His certainty that Allah was watching"],["Mukhlisin","Sincere servants"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the verb rawadathu imply?","o":["A single moment of weakness","Repeated attempts that grew stronger","A misunderstanding"],"a":1,"w":"Repeated attempts, intensifying as he matured."},{"q":"Why is she called “the lady in whose house he lived”?","o":["To hide her identity","To show how hard the test was for Yusuf","Because she was not married"],"a":1,"w":"He lived under her authority, with no escape."},{"q":"How does ma‘adha-llah differ from a‘udhu billah?","o":["It stresses that Allah alone is the perfect refuge","It is a weaker phrase","It is only said in prayer"],"a":0,"w":"“The One whose refuge I seek is Allah.”"},{"q":"What is recorded for someone who intends an evil deed but does not do it?","o":["A sin","Nothing","A complete good deed"],"a":2,"w":"Muslim 235."},{"q":"What was the burhan, according to the strongest opinion?","o":["An angel at the door","His certainty that Allah was watching him","His father’s face in the window"],"a":1,"w":"Seven or eight opinions exist; this is the strongest."},{"q":"What links the two readings mukhlasin and mukhlisin?","o":["Sincerity is the way to be chosen","One of them is a mistake","They mean the same word in two dialects"],"a":0,"w":"Both are revelation, and both are true together."}]},
{"id":6,"verses":[25,29],"title":"The Shirt as Evidence","short":"Verses 25–29: a race to the door and a torn shirt","big":"They race to the door: he to escape sin, she to commit it. She tears his shirt from behind, finds her husband at the door, and instantly blames Yusuf. A witness from her own family proposes a test: look at where the shirt is torn. The evidence clears Yusuf, but al-‘Aziz, protecting his reputation, tells him to forget it.","remember":["Two people ran the same way with opposite intentions: do not judge an outward act until you know the intention.","Blaming the victim is worse than the crime itself (4:112), and a trait of Iblis (7:16).","Protecting your honour is right, but not obligatory against every accusation.","A torn back meant he was running away and she was pulling him back.","Ibn Taymiyyah and Ibn al-Qayyim: strong circumstantial evidence can suffice.","Al-‘Aziz chose damage control: pride makes people tolerate corruption at home if it stays private."],"more":[{"t":"At the door","l":["Alfaya, “they found”, implies the husband was not expected, which is why she had locked the doors.","Sayyidaha, “her master”: one might expect “his master” or “her husband”. The word stresses how deep her betrayal was.","She said “intended evil”, not that anything happened: nothing had, and a claim of an actual act would need stronger proof and could backfire."]},{"t":"One sin leads to another","l":["Like the brothers, she fell from one sin into the next: lust, then a lie, then blaming an innocent man.","“Whoever commits a sin and blames it on an innocent person bears slander and blatant sin” (4:112).","Iblis refused to prostrate, then blamed Allah: “You led me astray” (7:16)."]},{"t":"Yusuf defends himself","l":["Here, as a slave who could easily be jailed, he defended himself: “It was she who tried to seduce me.”","Later, as a minister, he stayed silent when accused, since there was no danger and replying would have revealed who he was.","Applied to Islam: rebutting attacks is sometimes obligatory, especially when Muslims are weak; when Islam is strong, some attacks need no reply.","A specific accusation is usually more truthful than a vague one. He also spoke about her to her husband, not to her, out of respect."]},{"t":"The witness and the test","l":["He did not see the event, yet he is called a witness: Allah is the true witness, he stated a sound rule, and he may have seen her conduct towards Yusuf over time.","Min ahliha: the soundest view is that he was her relative, so no one could call him biased against her.","A tear at the front would mean she was defending herself; a tear at the back, that he was fleeing.","The majority require two witnesses (four for zina). This verse supports those who accept strong circumstantial evidence, such as fingerprints and DNA today."]},{"t":"Shirts, racing, and fitrah","l":["The brothers said they were racing, and brought a bloodied but untorn shirt as false evidence. Now there is a race again, and a torn shirt saves Yusuf’s honour.","“Forget about this”: of the four in the room, only Yusuf might talk, so he was told to pretend it never happened.","Al-‘Aziz kept Yusuf in the house, underestimating her passion.","Though a pagan, he told her to seek forgiveness: revulsion at betraying a spouse is built into human nature."]}],"home":"The next time you are about to judge someone by what they did, ask yourself what their intention might have been, and what the evidence really shows.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Speak up, or stay silent?","help":"Does each describe the first accusation (as a slave) or the second (as a minister)?","buckets":["First accusation: a slave","Second accusation: a minister"],"items":[["He could easily be jailed",0],["He defended himself",0],["“It was she who tried to seduce me”",0],["There was no threat of prison",1],["He stayed silent",1],["Replying would have revealed who he was",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What lesson comes from “they raced to the door”?","o":["The fastest person is right","Do not judge an outward act until you know the intention","Always lock your doors"],"a":1,"w":"Same direction, opposite intentions."},{"q":"Why did she accuse him only of “intending” evil?","o":["Nothing had happened, and a stronger claim needed stronger proof","She wanted to protect him","She did not know the word"],"a":0,"w":"A claim of an actual act could have backfired."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf defend himself here but stay silent later?","o":["He became afraid later","As a slave he could be jailed; as a minister there was no danger","He forgot the second time"],"a":1,"w":"Defending your honour is right, but not obligatory every time."},{"q":"Why was the witness’s judgement hard to dispute?","o":["He was a judge","He was from her own family","He saw everything"],"a":1,"w":"He could not be called biased against her."},{"q":"A shirt torn from the back showed that…","o":["She was defending herself","He was running away and she pulled him back","They were fighting"],"a":1,"w":"Verse 27."},{"q":"Why did al-‘Aziz respond so lightly?","o":["He did not believe the evidence","To protect his reputation","He wanted Yusuf punished later"],"a":1,"w":"Damage control: pride tolerates private corruption."}]},
{"id":7,"verses":[30,35],"title":"Gossip, the Banquet and Prison","short":"Verses 30–35: “Prison is dearer to me”","big":"News of the scandal spreads among the women of the city. She invites them to a banquet, and on seeing Yusuf they cut their hands. She openly threatens him with prison. Yusuf turns to Allah: prison is dearer to him than sin. Allah answers him, and though they know he is innocent, they imprison him to quiet the scandal.","remember":["Ghibah is speaking of someone in a way they would dislike, even if true; buhtan is a lie; namimah is passing gossip on to cause harm.","When a gathering turns to gossip, defend the absent person, or at least stay silent and dislike it.","Fa-sta‘sam, “he firmly refused”: his inner beauty matched his outer beauty.","“Prison is dearer to me”: prefer worldly hardship over sin.","Du‘a is the believer’s weapon: “His Lord answered him.”","Life is a ladder of tests: well, luxury, prison… and for those who pass, Jannah."],"more":[{"t":"Gossip spreads","l":["It is Allah’s Sunnah that gossip spreads and grows. Most scholars say it leaked through the household slaves, or her passion was simply too obvious to hide.","Shaghafaha hubban: shaghaf is the innermost lining of the heart. Her love had reached its core.","Turawidu, in the present tense, implies she had not succeeded, adding to her humiliation."]},{"t":"Forbidden speech","l":["Ghibah, backbiting, is among the sins punished in the grave. Buhtan, slander, is worse.","Namimah, carrying tales, is passing gossip on to cause harm: “The tale-bearer shall not enter Paradise” (Muslim 196), even if he did not start it.","Cover sins, especially indecent ones. The exception: a victim of a crime has every right to expose it and involve the law."]},{"t":"The banquet","l":["They mocked her helplessness before a slave, so she decided to show them why.","Muttaka’ is a place of reclining. She served a meal and gave each a knife to cut fruit; on seeing Yusuf they cut their hands without feeling it, testifying to his beauty with their blood.","“This is a noble angel”: even pagans believed angels are beautiful.","His beauty was not relative: every one of them, married women included, lost her senses. Illicit desire is one of the hardest human tests."]},{"t":"Inner beauty, and contentment","l":["She confessed openly: “I did try to seduce him, but he firmly refused,” then threatened him with prison and disgrace.","People are drawn to inner beauty too, and for Muslims it matters more.","Allah gave Yusuf half of all beauty and tested him with it. Most of us would not withstand such a test: be content with what Allah has given you. He knows our weaknesses."]},{"t":"“Prison is dearer to me”","l":["Zina leads to the prison of the Hereafter, far worse and lasting. Offered the most alluring pleasure and threatened with the worst punishment, he chose prison out of fear of Allah and hope in His reward.","“Unless You turn their cunning from me”: he knew he could not resist without Allah’s help.","Al-jahilin: the height of ignorance is knowing the truth and acting against it. Early scholars said every sinner is a jahil (4:17).","“The signs” were most likely Yusuf’s character. They knew he was innocent, yet punished the victim to protect a powerful minister’s wife."]}],"home":"For one week, whenever a conversation turns to someone who is not there, either say something good about them or change the subject.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 30–35","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Ghibah","Backbiting, even if it is true"],["Buhtan","Slander: a blatant lie"],["Namimah","Carrying tales to cause harm"],["Shaghaf","The innermost lining of the heart"],["Fa-sta‘sam","He firmly refused and guarded himself"],["Al-jahilin","Those who know the truth and act against it"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What is ghibah?","o":["Telling a lie about someone","Speaking of someone in a way they would dislike, even if true","Praising someone too much"],"a":1,"w":"A lie would be buhtan, which is worse."},{"q":"What should you do when a gathering turns to gossip?","o":["Join in quietly","Defend the absent person, or at least stay silent","Leave and tell them what was said"],"a":1,"w":"Telling them would be namimah."},{"q":"Why did the women cut their hands?","o":["They were careless cooks","They were overwhelmed by Yusuf’s beauty","She told them to"],"a":1,"w":"They testified to his beauty with their blood."},{"q":"Why was prison dearer to Yusuf?","o":["Prison was comfortable","Sin leads to the far worse prison of the Hereafter","He wanted to escape his work"],"a":1,"w":"Out of fear of Allah and hope in His reward."},{"q":"What does “every sinner is a jahil” mean?","o":["Sinners cannot read","Acting against the truth you know is the height of ignorance","Only the uneducated sin"],"a":1,"w":"Linked to 4:17."},{"q":"Why was Yusuf imprisoned?","o":["He was found guilty","To quiet the scandal, though they knew he was innocent","He asked to go"],"a":1,"w":"They punished the victim to protect the powerful."}]},
/* ---------- Part 4: The Prisoner ---------- */
{"id":8,"verses":[36,42],"title":"Da‘wah in the Prison","short":"Verses 36–42: two dreams, and “mention me”","big":"In prison, two of the king’s servants ask Yusuf about their dreams. Before answering, he calls them to Allah, gently and without compromise, starting with tawhid. He interprets the dreams, and asks the one who will be freed to mention him to the king. Shaytan makes the man forget, and Yusuf stays in prison for several more years.","remember":["Yusuf built trust through his conduct first: “We see you are one of the good-doers.”","“From what my Lord taught me”: a believer attributes every good to Allah.","Tawhid first, as the Prophet ﷺ taught Mu‘adh before sending him to Yemen (Muslim 19).","Criticise the action, not the person; be warm, and never compromise the message.","Tawakkul means relying on Allah and taking the means: asking for help is allowed.","Bid‘ sinin is three to nine years; most say seven more years, a delay full of wisdom."],"more":[{"t":"Two dreams in prison","l":["They were the king’s winemaker and breadmaker, accused of plotting to poison the king.","Even in prison, Yusuf’s character and knowledge set him apart. They understood that a righteous man could interpret dreams.","“Before your next meal arrives”: he put them at ease, then used the moment to speak about Allah. Spread the message in whatever situation you find yourself."]},{"t":"Tawhid first","l":["He explained how he gained this gift: by leaving the faith of people who disbelieve in Allah and the Hereafter.","Mu‘adh was told: call them first to the testimony of faith; if they accept, teach the five prayers; then zakah. Wisdom is teaching the most important first.","“I follow the faith of my fathers Ibrahim, Ishaq and Ya‘qub”: the same religion as every prophet (21:25). Min shay’, “anything at all”: nothing whatsoever is associated with Allah.","In a few sentences he covered tawhid, risalah and the Hereafter."]},{"t":"Gratitude in prison","l":["Taken from his father, enslaved, falsely accused and imprisoned, he still said: “This is Allah’s grace upon us and on humanity.”","He focused on his blessings, not his hardships. Look at Allah’s countless blessings rather than complaining about a few trials."]},{"t":"“O my two fellow-prisoners”","l":["A warm address, built on shared experience. The Qur’an invites with “O mankind” and “O children of Adam”; harsh address appears only when disbelievers ask the Prophet ﷺ to compromise tawhid (109:1–2, 39:64).","“Are many lords better, or Allah, the One, the Supreme?”: direct but noble, appealing to their fitrah. Al-Wahid has no equal; al-Qahhar overpowers everything, mightier than any king.","“Mere names you invented… judgement belongs only to Allah… worship none but Him”: polite but uncompromising. Not all religions are equal; only what Allah has authorised is the upright religion.","Together these cover all three aspects of tawhid: Lordship, Names and Attributes, and worship."]},{"t":"The interpretation, and “mention me”","l":["The Qur’an does not say whether they accepted Islam: our goal is to present the truth well, without bending it, and leave the outcome to Allah.","He said “one of you”, delivering the bad news gently. The winemaker would serve the king again; the breadmaker would be executed. “The matter is decided”: only a prophet speaks with such certainty.","“Mention me to your master”: the Prophet ﷺ also sought protection, made alliances and signed treaties. Using every lawful means, even the courts, is Sunnah.","Some early authors said Shaytan made Yusuf forget Allah and he was punished for asking a person. The author calls this simply false: it rests on a wrong idea of tawakkul.","“Victory comes with patience, relief with affliction, and ease with hardship” (Ahmad 2800)."]}],"home":"Pick one person you could help understand Islam. Start with good character, speak warmly, and begin with who Allah is. Then make du‘a and take one practical step, leaving the outcome to Allah.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Yusuf’s way of calling to Allah","help":"Is each something Yusuf did, or something he avoided?","buckets":["What Yusuf did","What he avoided"],"items":[["Building trust through his conduct",0],["Putting them at ease first",0],["Starting with tawhid",0],["Addressing them warmly",0],["Attacking them personally",1],["Compromising tawhid to win them over",1],["Claiming the gift as his own genius",1],["Naming who would get the bad news",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Who were the two young men in prison?","o":["Yusuf’s brothers","The king’s winemaker and breadmaker","Two travellers from Canaan"],"a":1,"w":"Accused of plotting to poison the king."},{"q":"What did Yusuf talk about before interpreting their dreams?","o":["His own story","Allah and tawhid","The food in prison"],"a":1,"w":"Tawhid first, the priority of every prophet."},{"q":"What does the hadith of Mu‘adh teach about da‘wah?","o":["Teach everything at once","Teach the most important first","Only teach those who ask"],"a":1,"w":"Testimony of faith, then prayer, then zakah."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf say “one of you” rather than name the man?","o":["He did not know","To deliver the bad news gently","It was a riddle"],"a":1,"w":"He softened the blow."},{"q":"Was it wrong of Yusuf to ask, “Mention me to your master”?","o":["Yes, he should only have made du‘a","No: tawakkul includes taking the means","Yes, and he was punished for it"],"a":1,"w":"The author calls the punishment reading simply false."},{"q":"How many more years did Yusuf stay in prison, according to most scholars?","o":["One","Seven","Twenty"],"a":1,"w":"Bid‘ sinin means three to nine; most say seven."}]},
/* ---------- Part 5: The King and His Dream ---------- */
{"id":9,"verses":[43,45],"title":"Al-Malik and His Ru’ya","short":"Verses 43–45: the King’s dream","big":"While Yusuf is still in prison, the King of Egypt has a disturbing dream. His chiefs dismiss it as confused nonsense. Only then does the freed winemaker, years late, remember the man who interpreted his own dream.","remember":["In Yusuf’s story the ruler is always al-Malik, “the King”; in Musa’s story he is Fir‘awn, “Pharaoh”.","Yusuf lived under the Hyksos, foreign rulers who did not use the title Pharaoh.","The Qur’an’s precision here, where the Bible calls both rulers Pharaoh, is a proof that it is from Allah.","Ru’ya is a meaningful, true dream; adghath ahlam are jumbled dreams with no deeper meaning.","The ignorant rarely admit ignorance: they justify it with made-up excuses.","Ba‘da ummah, “after a time”, implies the winemaker should have remembered long before."],"more":[{"t":"The King’s dream","l":["Seven fat cows eaten by seven thin ones, seven green ears of grain and seven dry ones.","The dream shook the King, and he called on his chiefs: “Tell me the meaning, if you can interpret dreams.”"]},{"t":"The King, not Pharaoh","l":["In Yusuf’s time Egypt was ruled by the Hyksos, foreign invaders, who did not call themselves Pharaohs. In Musa’s time a native Egyptian dynasty ruled, and its kings did.","The Qur’an calls the first al-Malik and the second Fir‘awn. The Bible calls both Pharaoh.","Some scholars place Yusuf in Manf (Memphis)."]},{"t":"A proof of revelation","l":["The Pharaonic era had ended long before the Prophet ﷺ was born, and nothing around him could have taught him this distinction.","Getting such a detail right, where other sources erred, points to a source that knows the unseen past."]},{"t":"Ru’ya or ahlam?","l":["The King called it a ru’ya: he sensed his dream mattered.","His chiefs called it adghath ahlam, “confused dreams”, and said they could not interpret “such dreams”.","That was saving face: “if it were a real dream, we could interpret it.” Admitting “I don’t know” is a mark of honesty and of real knowledge."]},{"t":"The winemaker remembers","l":["When word spread that no minister could interpret the dream, the winemaker finally remembered Yusuf.","He saw a chance to be noticed by the King, and asked to be sent to the prison."]}],"home":"The next time you are asked something you do not know, practise saying “I don’t know” or “Allah knows best”, without making an excuse.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"The King or Pharaoh?","help":"Does each describe the time of Yusuf or the time of Musa?","buckets":["Time of Yusuf","Time of Musa"],"items":[["Foreign rulers, the Hyksos",0],["The Qur’an says al-Malik, “the King”",0],["Rulers who did not use the title Pharaoh",0],["A native Egyptian dynasty",1],["The Qur’an says Fir‘awn",1],["Rulers who called themselves Pharaohs",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Qur’an call the ruler of Egypt in Yusuf’s story?","o":["Fir‘awn","Al-Malik, the King","Al-‘Aziz"],"a":1,"w":"In Musa’s story he is Fir‘awn."},{"q":"Why was the ruler in Yusuf’s time not a Pharaoh?","o":["Egypt had no ruler","He was one of the Hyksos, foreign rulers who did not use the title","He refused the title out of humility"],"a":1,"w":"Pharaoh was the title of the native dynasties."},{"q":"Why is this detail a sign the Qur’an is from Allah?","o":["The Prophet ﷺ had studied Egyptian history","The Pharaonic era had ended long before, and he could not have known it","Every book says the same"],"a":1,"w":"Even the Bible calls both rulers Pharaoh."},{"q":"What is the difference between ru’ya and adghath ahlam?","o":["A meaningful dream versus jumbled dreams","A long dream versus a short one","A daytime dream versus a night dream"],"a":0,"w":"The King called it a ru’ya; the chiefs dismissed it."},{"q":"Why did the chiefs call the dream “confused”?","o":["It truly meant nothing","To save face instead of admitting ignorance","The King asked them to"],"a":1,"w":"The ignorant rarely admit ignorance."},{"q":"What does ba‘da ummah imply about the winemaker?","o":["He remembered quickly","He should have remembered long before","He never met Yusuf"],"a":1,"w":"“After a time”: years too late."}]},
{"id":10,"verses":[46,49],"title":"Seven Years of Plenty, Seven of Drought","short":"Verses 46–49: an interpretation and a plan","big":"The winemaker comes to Yusuf in prison without a word of apology. Yusuf does not blame him. He interprets the dream, then goes further: he gives a fifteen-year plan to save Egypt, for idol-worshippers who had wronged him, and the King is left asking, “Who is this man?”","remember":["Al-siddiq, “man of truth”: the winemaker had seen Yusuf’s honesty and accuracy in prison.","Yusuf held no grudge against the man who forgot him for seven years.","He gave the solution, not only the meaning: go beyond what is asked.","Seven good years: store the grain in its ears, eating only a little. Seven hard years: eat from the store, keeping seed.","The fifteenth year of rain is not in the dream: Yusuf deduced it.","He saved the lives of people who had wronged him: be merciful, and receive Allah’s mercy."],"more":[{"t":"No grudge","l":["Seven years had passed, and the winemaker did not apologise. Yusuf did not scold him or send him away.","He also never told Yusuf the dream was the King’s, perhaps wanting the credit and a reward for himself. Yusuf was above such petty games.","“So that I may return to the people” would, in Allah’s plan, also make Yusuf’s skill and plight known."]},{"t":"Answer and solution","l":["Yusuf interpreted the dream and gave the solution: the height of good manners and sincerity to Allah.","Unthreshed grain keeps longer, so he said: leave what you harvest in its ears.","From a prison cell, Allah placed him in a position to shape the King’s policy."]},{"t":"No conditions","l":["The Prophet ﷺ said: “I am amazed at the patience of my brother Yusuf and his generosity… When he was asked to interpret the dream, I would not have done so until they let me out” (Tabarani, al-Mu‘jam al-Kabir 1945).","Yusuf did not say “free me first”. He helped first."]},{"t":"Fifteen years","l":["Years 1–7: plant and store, eating little. Years 8–14: drought and crop failure; live off the store and keep back seed.","Year 15: abundant rain, and people press oil and wine again. Nobody presses wine when there is not enough to eat, so it signals plenty.","The dream does not mention this year. A drought must end with rain, or it would not end: this shows the depth of his understanding."]},{"t":"Mercy, and Allah’s plan","l":["He gave life-saving advice to idol-worshippers who were also his oppressors. He valued their lives as fellow humans.","The interpretation filled the King with curiosity. Allah’s plan was bringing “the boy in the well” to the King’s court.","When Allah is on your side, help comes from sources no one could predict."]}],"home":"Help someone this week without waiting for them to thank you, apologise, or do something for you first, even someone who once let you down.","game":{"type":"order","title":"From the prison to the plan","help":"Tap the events in the order they come in the surah.","items":["The winemaker remembers Yusuf","He visits Yusuf in prison","He calls Yusuf “O man of truth”","Yusuf explains the dream","Seven good years: store grain in its ears","Seven hard years: eat from the store, keep seed","A year of abundant rain"]},"quiz":[{"q":"How did Yusuf treat the winemaker who had forgotten him?","o":["He refused to help","He helped without blaming him","He demanded an apology"],"a":1,"w":"Don’t hold grudges."},{"q":"What did the Prophet ﷺ say he would have done in Yusuf’s place?","o":["Refused to interpret until he was freed","Interpreted the dream straight away","Asked for gold"],"a":0,"w":"He admired Yusuf’s patience and generosity."},{"q":"Why store the grain in its ears?","o":["It looks better","Unthreshed grain keeps longer","The King liked it that way"],"a":1,"w":"Practical advice for the hard years."},{"q":"What should be kept back during the seven hard years?","o":["Seed for planting","Wine","Gold"],"a":0,"w":"So farming could start again."},{"q":"Where did the year of abundant rain come from?","o":["The King’s dream","Yusuf deduced it","The winemaker"],"a":1,"w":"A drought must end with rain."},{"q":"Who did Yusuf’s plan save?","o":["Only the believers","Everyone, including those who wronged him","Only the King’s family"],"a":1,"w":"Be merciful, and receive Allah’s mercy."}]},
/* ---------- Part 6: From Prisoner to Minister ---------- */
{"id":11,"verses":[50,53],"title":"Exoneration","short":"Verses 50–53: the truth comes out","big":"The King sends for Yusuf, but Yusuf refuses to leave prison until his name is cleared. The King questions the women, they declare Yusuf innocent, and the wife of al-‘Aziz confesses: “Now the truth has come out.” She admits that the soul inclines to evil, and hopes for Allah’s forgiveness.","remember":["Honour over freedom: being accused of immorality hurt Yusuf more than prison.","He said “the women who cut their hands”: discreet, and clever enough to make the King investigate.","Hashasa al-haqq: “now the truth is clear”. The truth eventually comes out.","Nafs ammarah incites to evil; nafs lawwamah reproaches itself; nafs mutma’innah is at peace.","Another valid view: they are three states of one soul.","“Every child is born upon the fitrah” (Bukhari 1292): no one is created evil."],"more":[{"t":"Refusing release","l":["Anyone else would have rushed out after years in prison. The Prophet ﷺ admired this patience.","Egyptian society did not treat the accusation as serious, yet Yusuf wanted it removed from his record.","He did not name the wife of al-‘Aziz or go into detail. The cryptic wording made the King curious enough to find the truth himself, and it worked."]},{"t":"The King investigates","l":["Without the dream, the King would never have looked at such a “petty” case. Now it reached Egypt’s highest court, and the King had a personal interest in it.","“Hasha li-llah!”, Allah forbid, expresses shock at being suspected. Then the women defended Yusuf: “We know nothing bad about him.”"]},{"t":"Her confession","l":["Everyone had testified; only she was left. She could not contradict them.","Almost a decade had passed, and some scholars say al-‘Aziz had died. She put it on record: “It was I who tried to seduce him, and he is truthful.”","Verse 52 is most reliably her speech about her husband: she planned the sin but never committed it. She confessed to the attempt and denied the act. Others said Yusuf is the speaker, but the context does not support it.","Verse 53: years of guilt, an innocent man jailed, and now she clears her conscience, hoping for forgiveness."]},{"t":"Three kinds of soul","l":["An-nafs al-ammarah (12:53): without faith it has no compass, and commits evil without remorse. This is the soul she means.","An-nafs al-lawwamah (75:2): it has faith but is not settled. It swings between faith and desire, and blames itself after sinning. Most Muslims are here.","An-nafs al-mutma’innah (89:27–28): the content believer, at peace with its Lord, who does not incline to evil and deserves the highest reward.","That is why we sometimes feel at peace, sometimes regret, and sometimes desire evil."]},{"t":"Fitrah","l":["Every child is born on the fitrah. No one is created evil.","With faith, the soul inclines to good; when faith leaves the heart, it inclines to evil. Faith moves the soul towards peace."]}],"home":"At the end of each day this week, check your nafs: when did you feel at peace, when did you regret something, and when did you want something wrong? Ask Allah’s forgiveness for the last.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 50–53","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Hasha li-llah","Allah forbid!"],["Hashasa al-haqq","Now the truth is clear"],["An-nafs al-ammarah","The soul that incites to evil"],["An-nafs al-lawwamah","The soul that reproaches itself"],["An-nafs al-mutma’innah","The soul at peace"],["Fitrah","The pure nature every child is born with"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Yusuf refuse to leave prison straight away?","o":["He was afraid of the King","He wanted his name cleared first","He liked prison"],"a":1,"w":"Honour mattered more to him than freedom."},{"q":"Why did he mention “the women who cut their hands”?","o":["To be discreet and make the King investigate","He had forgotten her name","To blame the women"],"a":0,"w":"It worked: the King found the truth himself."},{"q":"What does hashasa al-haqq mean?","o":["Now the truth is clear","Allah forbid","Go back to your master"],"a":0,"w":"Her confession."},{"q":"In verse 52, who is most reliably speaking?","o":["Yusuf","The wife of al-‘Aziz","The King"],"a":1,"w":"She says she never betrayed her husband in the act."},{"q":"Which soul blames itself after sinning?","o":["An-nafs al-ammarah","An-nafs al-lawwamah","An-nafs al-mutma’innah"],"a":1,"w":"Most Muslims are here (75:2)."},{"q":"What does “every child is born upon the fitrah” teach?","o":["No one is created evil","Children cannot sin","Faith is not needed"],"a":0,"w":"Bukhari 1292."}]},
{"id":12,"verses":[54,57],"title":"Over the Storehouses of the Land","short":"Verses 54–57: makin, amin, hafiz, ‘alim","big":"Convinced of Yusuf’s innocence before even meeting him, the King takes him as a close adviser: “highly placed and fully trusted”. Yusuf asks to manage Egypt’s storehouses, since he knows the crisis ahead. Allah establishes him in the land, and reminds us that the reward of the Hereafter is better still.","remember":["The first “bring him to me” was curiosity; the second, “for myself”, was conviction.","Makin: honoured and established in rank. Amin: trustworthy.","Hafiz: a trustworthy guardian. ‘Alim: knowledgeable and skilled. Success needs both.","A Muslim may serve in a non-Muslim system to benefit society, within limits that protect faith.","“We never waste the reward of the good-doers.”","The Hereafter is better: believers are rewarded twice."],"more":[{"t":"The King’s trust","l":["The investigation had convinced the King of Yusuf’s innocence and nobility, so he made him a close adviser and offered him a cabinet post.","The full cycle: a father’s love, the well, slavery, false accusation, prison, the King’s inner circle.","Every new trial was bigger than the last, but the victory was twice as sweet. Patience and sincerity paid off."]},{"t":"Hafiz and ‘alim","l":["From the dream, Yusuf knew the crisis ahead, so he asked to manage Egypt’s grain stores.","Hafiz: a guardian who protects what is in his care. ‘Alim: competent and skilled in the job.","This is the right balance of inner character and worldly skill. Character without skill, or skill without character, falls short."]},{"t":"Working in a non-Muslim government","l":["Yusuf asked for a ministry in a non-Muslim government in order to help people.","It is permissible as long as it benefits the community and does not harm one’s religious practice or beliefs.","Working within a flawed system is not endorsing it. Scholars of each place and time should set the limits."]},{"t":"Established in the land","l":["“Thus We established Yusuf in the land” appears for the second time; the first was when he reached al-‘Aziz’s house as a child (12:21).","He could settle wherever he wished. Controlling the food supply made him in some ways more powerful than the King: everyone needs food, not everyone needs a king.","For seven years people handed in their produce, and the surplus was stored for the seven years of drought.","Why was he rewarded? His righteousness. Allah never lets the good deeds of the righteous go to waste."]},{"t":"The reward of the Hereafter","l":["Power, wealth and privilege are short-lived. Do not be fooled by worldly status.","Sufyan ibn ‘Uyaynah and other early scholars drew from verse 57 that believers are rewarded in this world and again in the Hereafter.","Disbelievers may be rewarded in this world for beneficial work, but have no share in the eternal reward, a loss they will realise only on the Day of Judgement."]}],"home":"Pick one responsibility you hold, at home, school or work. Ask yourself: am I hafiz (trustworthy with it) and ‘alim (skilled at it)? Choose one step to improve the weaker one.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Hafiz or ‘alim?","help":"Is each one about character (hafiz) or skill (‘alim)?","buckets":["Hafiz: trustworthy guardian","‘Alim: knowledge and skill"],"items":[["Protecting what is in his care",0],["Honest when nobody is watching",0],["Never taking what is not his",0],["Planning ahead for the drought",1],["Knowing how to store grain",1],["Being skilled at the job",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What changed between the King’s two “Bring him to me”s?","o":["Nothing","Curiosity became conviction of Yusuf’s innocence and nobility","The King became angry"],"a":1,"w":"The second time he wanted Yusuf “for myself”."},{"q":"What do makin and amin mean?","o":["Rich and famous","Honoured in rank, and trustworthy","Strong and clever"],"a":1,"w":"The King’s words to Yusuf."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf ask for the storehouses?","o":["To become rich","He knew the crisis ahead from the dream","The King insisted"],"a":1,"w":"He could save Egypt from the famine."},{"q":"Hafiz and ‘alim together mean…","o":["Character and competence","Wealth and power","Youth and beauty"],"a":0,"w":"A person needs both to succeed."},{"q":"When may a Muslim work in a non-Muslim government?","o":["Never","When it benefits society and does not harm their faith","Only if they become the ruler"],"a":1,"w":"Working within a flawed system is not endorsing it."},{"q":"What did early scholars draw from verse 57?","o":["Believers are rewarded in this world and again in the Hereafter","Only the rich are rewarded","Worldly reward is the only reward"],"a":0,"w":"The Hereafter is better and lasting."}]},
/* ---------- Part 7: The Brothers Return ---------- */
{"id":13,"verses":[58,62],"title":"The Brothers Before Al-‘Aziz","short":"Verses 58–62: he knew them; they did not know him","big":"Famine drives Yusuf’s brothers to Egypt for grain. They stand before the minister without recognising him. Yusuf hosts them generously, asks them to bring their youngest brother next time, warns them they will get nothing without him, and secretly returns their goods so they can come back.","remember":["Four words, “and Yusuf’s brothers came”, carry years of story: a miracle of Qur’anic concision.","Thanks to Yusuf’s planning, Egypt had no starvation in the famine, even a surplus.","He recognised them; they had last seen a boy of about seven, and could never imagine him as Egypt’s minister.","Incentive first, then warning: “I give full measure and am the best of hosts”, then “no grain without him”.","“His father”, not “our father”: the old jealousy was still there.","Returning their goods was within his authority: Yusuf would never abuse public funds."],"more":[{"t":"The missing scenes","l":["It was probably the sixth year of famine. Drought had spread to Palestine, and people travelled far to Egypt for grain.","The King now saw in practice what “I am a knowing guardian” (12:55) meant.","Ordinary traders did not deal with the minister. The brothers did because they were foreigners buying a huge quantity for a large clan."]},{"t":"Why only he recognised them","l":["They were older, wore the same dress, and spoke his native Hebrew.","They had last seen him as a little boy. That he had survived, let alone risen to be Egypt’s second most powerful man, was unimaginable.","Scholars say he questioned them until he had full news of his family: brothers in Palestine and an elderly father."]},{"t":"Carrot, then stick","l":["He hosted them for some days with food, drink and lodging, and gave extra provisions for the journey.","“Bring me your brother from your father”: in effect, “you say there is an eleventh brother; bring him next time.”","Then the warning: without him, no grain and no audience.","Why take Binyamin from his father? Most logically, Allah inspired it as part of His plan. Possible wisdoms: to affirm Ya‘qub’s faith, and to protect Binyamin from mistreatment."]},{"t":"The returned goods","l":["Trade was mostly barter. Yusuf had their goods slipped back into their saddlebags with the grain.","It gave them a reason to return, since once they left he had no power over them, and he knew his family was poor and might not be able to come back otherwise.","It was ethical: either the King gave him authority to give grain freely, or he repaid the treasury from his own money."]}],"home":"Next time you need someone to do something, try encouragement and generosity before warnings. Notice which works better.","game":{"type":"order","title":"The first visit","help":"Tap the events in the order they come in the surah.","items":["Famine brings the brothers to Egypt","They enter the minister’s presence","Yusuf recognises them, but they do not know him","He hosts them and gives full measure","He asks them to bring their brother","He warns: no grain without him","He has their goods put back in their bags"]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did the brothers deal with the minister himself?","o":["They were his friends","They were foreigners buying a huge amount","Every trader did"],"a":1,"w":"They needed higher authorisation."},{"q":"Why did the brothers not recognise Yusuf?","o":["He wore a mask","They had last seen a little boy, and could not imagine him a minister","He spoke to them through a servant"],"a":1,"w":"He recognised them; they could not imagine it was him."},{"q":"What came first: the incentive or the warning?","o":["The warning","The incentive","Neither"],"a":1,"w":"“I give full measure and am the best of hosts,” then the warning."},{"q":"What does “his father” instead of “our father” suggest?","o":["They were not really brothers","The old jealousy was still there","It is a translation error"],"a":1,"w":"They still resented Ya‘qub’s love for Binyamin."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf return their goods?","o":["By accident","To give them a reason and the means to come back","To test their honesty only"],"a":1,"w":"He knew the family was poor."},{"q":"Was returning the goods a misuse of public money?","o":["Yes","No: it was within his authority, or he paid it himself","Nobody knows"],"a":1,"w":"Yusuf would never abuse his power."}]},
{"id":14,"verses":[63,66],"title":"Should I Trust You Again?","short":"Verses 63–66: a familiar promise, and an oath by Allah","big":"Back home, the brothers press their father to send Binyamin, using the very words they once used about Yusuf: “we will guard him.” Ya‘qub will not be stung twice, but the returned goods and the need for food persuade him. He sends Binyamin only after a solemn oath by Allah, and places his trust in Allah, the best Guardian.","remember":["“Wa inna lahu la-hafizun”: the exact words they had used about Yusuf decades before.","“The believer is not stung twice from the same hole” (Bukhari 6133).","“Allah is the best Guardian”: Ya‘qub turned to Allah by one of His Names.","A ruined reputation is hard to rebuild; how much more do we need good credentials for Jannah?","An unspoken mutual understanding can complete a transaction, and giving a buyer extra is allowed.","Oaths by Allah are sacred (3:77). Ya‘qub allowed only for what was truly beyond their control."],"more":[{"t":"A familiar promise","l":["They had barely unpacked before pressing him: the family was in real need.","“We will certainly guard him” must have struck Ya‘qub like a thunderbolt.","“Should I trust you with him as I trusted you with his brother before?” His love for Yusuf had not faded, and he still held them responsible."]},{"t":"Reputation","l":["The brothers were not trusted because their reputation was ruined.","If reputation matters so much in this world, how much more does it matter for entry into Jannah, which needs far better credentials?"]},{"t":"“What more could we want?”","l":["Finding their goods returned, they knew it was no accident and used it to press their case.","Ma nabghi carries two meanings: “what more could we want?” and “we mean no harm”.","Yusuf’s rationing was one camel-load per person, so another brother meant another load.","Fiqh: some scholars require offer and acceptance to be spoken or written, but this shows an unspoken understanding can suffice when both sides clearly agree."]},{"t":"A solemn oath","l":["Yusuf’s generous treatment probably weighed most in persuading Ya‘qub.","“Those who trade Allah’s covenant and their oaths for a small price will have no share in the Hereafter” (3:77). As a prophet, Ya‘qub had taught his sons this.","“Unless you are overwhelmed”: he allowed for circumstances truly beyond their control.","“Allah is Witness over what we say”: the oath placed the matter in Allah’s hands."]}],"home":"Think of one way you have let someone down before. What would rebuild their trust in you? Do one small, reliable thing towards it this week.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 63–66","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["La-hafizun","“We will guard him”: their old promise about Yusuf"],["Stung twice","A believer learns from experience"],["Al-Hafiz","Allah, the best Guardian"],["Ma nabghi","“What more could we want?”"],["A camel-load","The ration for each person"],["Mawthiq","A solemn oath by Allah"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did “we will guard him” shock Ya‘qub?","o":["It was rude","It was what they had promised about Yusuf","He did not understand it"],"a":1,"w":"The exact same words, decades earlier."},{"q":"What does “the believer is not stung twice from the same hole” teach?","o":["Avoid all risks","Learn from experience","Never trust anyone"],"a":1,"w":"Bukhari 6133."},{"q":"By which Name did Ya‘qub turn to Allah?","o":["Al-Hafiz, the best Guardian","Al-Malik, the King","Al-Qahhar, the Supreme"],"a":0,"w":"He placed his tawakkul in Allah."},{"q":"Why was the extra camel-load significant?","o":["Rations were one load per person","Camels were cheap","It was a gift for Yusuf"],"a":0,"w":"Another brother meant another load."},{"q":"What fiqh point does the returned merchandise show?","o":["Every sale must be written down","An unspoken understanding can suffice when both clearly agree","Gifts are forbidden in trade"],"a":1,"w":"Giving the buyer extra is permissible if voluntary."},{"q":"What does “unless you are overwhelmed” allow for?","o":["Any excuse","Circumstances truly beyond their control","Breaking the oath if they wish"],"a":1,"w":"Oaths by Allah are sacred."}]},
{"id":15,"verses":[67,68],"title":"Separate Gates, and Tawakkul","short":"Verses 67–68: the evil eye, and trusting Allah","big":"Ya‘qub tells his sons to enter the city by separate gates, both to avoid suspicion and to guard against the evil eye, and in the same breath affirms that nothing avails against Allah’s decree. His sons obey him, but the decree still unfolds. Ya‘qub had knowledge, understood it, and acted on it.","remember":["Two reasons for separate gates: eleven foreign men together would arouse suspicion, and could attract the evil eye.","“The evil eye is real” (Muslim 2188). Its root is envy.","When something impresses you, pray for blessing in it: say “ma sha’ Allah” and “tabaraka-llah”.","Recite al-Falaq, and do not show off your blessings.","Tawakkul: plan well, do your best, then leave the outcome to Allah.","Knowing, understanding and acting are rising levels of knowledge; acting is the highest."],"more":[{"t":"Why separate gates?","l":["Practical: walled cities had gates as checkpoints. Eleven foreign men of a different language, faith and customs entering together would stand out and might seem threatening.","Spiritual: eleven young, strong, handsome sons were a great blessing, and could attract al-‘ayn, the evil eye."]},{"t":"The evil eye: reality, not superstition","l":["Evidence: “The evil eye is real” (Muslim 2188), and al-Falaq: “from the evil of an envier when he envies” (113:5).","It comes from looking at someone’s wealth, beauty or position with resentment. Envy in the heart alone can also harm.","The strongest view is that envy empowers Shaytan to harm the victim. Whoever causes it, even unintentionally, is sinful for letting jealousy go unchecked.","“Envy consumes good deeds as fire burns wood” (Ibn Majah 4210)."]},{"t":"The right reaction, and protection","l":["“If one of you sees something in his brother, himself or his wealth that impresses him, let him pray for blessing in it. The evil eye is true” (Ahmad 15273). Never wish others to lose a blessing.","Seek refuge with Allah and recite al-Falaq often. The Prophet ﷺ sought refuge in Allah’s perfect words “from every devil, poisonous pest and every harmful envious eye” (Bukhari).","Do not flaunt blessings. Say “ma sha’ Allah” as in the story of the two gardens (18:39).","If the source is known, they may make wudu’ and the water is used to wash the afflicted person (Muwatta’ of Malik)."]},{"t":"Tawakkul and decree","l":["“I cannot avail you anything against Allah… in Him I trust.” Tawakkul is not sitting back and saying “if Allah wills, it will happen”.","They obeyed their father even in his absence: despite their past, they still had iman and virtue.","The precaution was “a need in Ya‘qub’s soul which he fulfilled”: he did his best, but it did not change qadar."]},{"t":"“He had knowledge because We taught him”","l":["Four interpretations: Allah is the giver of knowledge; Ya‘qub is praised for understanding it; Ibn ‘Abbas said for acting on it; and he knew he could not protect his sons from Allah’s decree, yet did everything in his power.","“Most people do not know”: most lack what Ya‘qub had: knowledge, understanding, and the wisdom to apply it."]}],"home":"Each morning and evening this week, recite al-Falaq. When something of someone else’s impresses you, say “ma sha’ Allah, tabaraka-llah” out loud.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Protection or harm?","help":"Does each help protect against the evil eye, or feed it?","buckets":["Protects","Feeds the evil eye"],"items":[["Saying “ma sha’ Allah, tabaraka-llah”",0],["Reciting al-Falaq",0],["Praying for blessing in what impresses you",0],["Wishing someone loses a blessing",1],["Showing off your blessings",1],["Letting jealousy go unchecked",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What was the practical reason for separate gates?","o":["To save time","Eleven foreign men together would arouse suspicion","The main gate was closed"],"a":1,"w":"Gates were security checkpoints."},{"q":"What is the root of the evil eye?","o":["Bad luck","Envy","The stars"],"a":1,"w":"Looking at others’ blessings with resentment."},{"q":"What should you do when something of someone else’s impresses you?","o":["Stay silent","Pray for blessing in it","Look away and frown"],"a":1,"w":"Ahmad 15273."},{"q":"Which surah should be recited often for protection?","o":["Al-Falaq","Al-Kawthar","Al-‘Asr"],"a":0,"w":"“From the evil of an envier when he envies.”"},{"q":"What is tawakkul?","o":["Doing nothing and waiting","Planning well, doing your best, then leaving the outcome to Allah","Avoiding all plans"],"a":1,"w":"Precautions do not override qadar."},{"q":"According to Ibn ‘Abbas, what is the highest level of knowledge?","o":["Knowing","Understanding","Acting on it"],"a":2,"w":"Knowing, understanding, acting: rising levels."}]},
/* ---------- Part 8: Binyamin and the Ruse ---------- */
{"id":16,"verses":[69,72],"title":"The Siqayah in the Saddlebag","short":"Verses 69–72: “I am your brother”","big":"Yusuf draws Binyamin aside and reveals himself: “I am your brother, so do not grieve.” Then, by a plan from Allah, he places the King’s cup in Binyamin’s bag. A herald cries, “O caravan, you are thieves!” The brothers turn to face their accusers, and a reward is offered for the cup’s return.","remember":["“Inni ana akhuka”: double emphasis, “I really am your brother.”","“Do not grieve over what they used to do”: the brothers may have been mistreating Binyamin.","Why keep Binyamin? Allah’s plan (12:76), and to rescue him: both can be true.","Yusuf placed the cup himself; the herald chose the word “thieves”, and Yusuf never said it.","They turned towards their accusers and asked what was “missing”, not “stolen”: the reaction of the innocent.","A reward for finding a lost item (ja‘alah) and a guarantee (kafalah) are both valid."],"more":[{"t":"The reunion","l":["Imagine his joy at seeing Binyamin after so many years. Some say he split the brothers into small groups to host them, and so got Binyamin alone.","Binyamin was too young to remember Yusuf’s face, but he had surely heard the whole story from Ya‘qub.","Binyamin, the youngest and Yusuf’s only full brother, faced ten half-brothers who saw themselves as an ‘usbah, a strong group."]},{"t":"A legal obstacle","l":["Egyptian policy barred foreigners from living there permanently, so Yusuf needed a way to keep Binyamin.","Siqayah: the King’s drinking cup or, more accurately, the bowl used to measure grain; likely precious, a symbol of the King’s power to distribute food.","“He placed” is singular: Yusuf did it himself, since using servants would have exposed the plan."]},{"t":"Could a prophet call them thieves?","l":["A prophet cannot lie. First, they were thieves in a sense: they had stolen Yusuf from his father.","Second, Yusuf only said what was technically true, such as “the bowl is missing, and the Canaanite caravan was the last to receive grain”.","Adhdhana mu’adhdhin: a herald called out, and the herald chose the word “thieves” himself."]},{"t":"The reaction of the innocent","l":["They turned towards their accusers rather than running, as thieves would.","“What are you missing?”, tafqidun, not “what was stolen?”: “perhaps something is lost and you think we took it.”","Suwa‘ is a measuring vessel, related to the sa‘ used for zakat al-fitr. In a famine, a camel-load of food was worth more than money.","Ja‘alah: normally pay and work must be specified, but a reward for finding a lost item is an exception. Kafalah: “I am its guarantor”, a third party taking financial responsibility."]}],"home":"If someone younger in your family or class is being picked on, be the one who says, “Don’t be sad; I’m with you.”","game":{"type":"match","title":"Terms from verses 69–72","help":"Tap a term on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Inni ana akhuka","“I really am your brother”"],["Siqayah","The King’s cup or measuring bowl"],["Adhdhana mu’adhdhin","A herald called out"],["Tafqidun","“Missing”, not “stolen”"],["Ja‘alah","A reward for finding a lost item"],["Kafalah","Guaranteeing a payment"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What did Yusuf tell Binyamin?","o":["“You are a thief”","“I am your brother, so do not grieve”","“Go home to your father”"],"a":1,"w":"Double emphasis: I really am your brother."},{"q":"What might “do not grieve over what they used to do” suggest?","o":["The brothers had been mistreating Binyamin","Binyamin had lost his money","Binyamin was ill"],"a":0,"w":"Some scholars draw this from the wording."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf need a plan to keep Binyamin?","o":["Egypt barred foreigners from staying permanently","Binyamin refused to stay","The King disliked him"],"a":0,"w":"A legal obstacle."},{"q":"Who chose the word “thieves”?","o":["Yusuf","The herald","Binyamin"],"a":1,"w":"Yusuf never said “thief” or “stealing”."},{"q":"What does it show that they turned towards their accusers?","o":["They were guilty","They were innocent and unafraid","They wanted to fight"],"a":1,"w":"Thieves would panic and run."},{"q":"Which contract allows a reward for finding a lost item?","o":["Kafalah","Ja‘alah","Riba"],"a":1,"w":"An exception to specifying pay and work."}]},
{"id":17,"verses":[73,76],"title":"By the Law of Ya‘qub","short":"Verses 73–76: “Thus did We plan for Yusuf”","big":"The brothers swear they are no thieves and, asked to name the penalty, give the law of Ya‘qub: the one in whose bag it is found is kept as a slave. Yusuf searches their bags slowly, leaving Binyamin’s for last, and the cup comes out. Allah planned it: by their own words, Yusuf could keep his brother.","remember":["“Tallahi”: an oath. They appealed to their good conduct in Egypt, and they were right.","Theft is a major sin linked to corruption: “Allah curses the thief” (Bukhari 6799).","By asking the brothers to name the penalty, the Egyptians set aside their own law.","Under the law of Ya‘qub, a thief became the slave of the one he stole from.","Din al-Malik: Allah calls a king’s legal system a din. Din is a whole way of life, not just ritual.","“We raise in degrees whom We will; above every possessor of knowledge is one more knowing.”"],"more":[{"t":"The seriousness of theft","l":["Theft is linked with fasad, corruption. La‘nah, Allah’s curse, means removal from His mercy, the most frightening prospect.","Cutting off the hand, when its strict conditions are met, reflects how serious it is. Theft was a serious crime in Egypt too."]},{"t":"Two laws","l":["Egyptian law: a thief was whipped or repaid double, then expelled, since foreigners could not stay.","The law of Ya‘qub: the thief became the slave of the one he stole from.","Yusuf could not apply Allah’s shari‘ah in Egypt. Allah put it in the Egyptians’ hearts to ask the brothers, and the brothers themselves named the penalty that let Yusuf keep Binyamin."]},{"t":"Bag by bag","l":["Until now the crowd spoke. Now “he began”: Yusuf took charge, searching slowly and methodically, leaving Binyamin’s bag for last so as not to arouse suspicion.","The brothers were confident: whatever their past, they were too upright to steal. Then it came out of Binyamin’s bag. Utter shock.","“Thus did We plan for Yusuf. He could not have taken his brother under the King’s law, except that Allah willed.”"]},{"t":"Raised in degrees","l":["Allah calls a polytheist king’s legal system a “din”: din covers law and a whole way of life.","Rafi‘ al-darajat raises ranks through faith and knowledge: “Allah will raise those who believe and those given knowledge in degrees” (58:11). Yusuf had both.","“The superiority of the scholar over the worshipper is like the full moon over the other stars” (Tirmidhi 2682). Adam was shown superior to the angels through knowledge.","Above every knower is one more knowing, and above all is the All-Knowing."]}],"home":"Choose one area of beneficial knowledge to grow in this month, and set a small daily goal. Allah raises people in degrees through faith and knowledge.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Two laws","help":"Does each belong to Egyptian law or to the law of Ya‘qub?","buckets":["Egyptian law","The law of Ya‘qub"],"items":[["The thief is whipped",0],["The thief repays double",0],["The foreign thief is expelled",0],["The thief serves the one he stole from",1],["The penalty the brothers named",1],["The law that let Yusuf keep Binyamin",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What did the brothers appeal to when they swore they were not thieves?","o":["Their wealth","Their good conduct on past visits","Their father’s rank"],"a":1,"w":"And they were right: they had done nothing wrong in Egypt."},{"q":"What does la‘nah, Allah’s curse on the thief, mean?","o":["A fine","Removal from Allah’s mercy","A short prayer"],"a":1,"w":"Bukhari 6799."},{"q":"What was the penalty under the law of Ya‘qub?","o":["Whipping","The thief became the slave of the one he stole from","Exile"],"a":1,"w":"The brothers named it themselves."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf search Binyamin’s bag last?","o":["He forgot it","To avoid suspicion","It was the biggest"],"a":1,"w":"Slowly and methodically, bag by bag."},{"q":"What does “din al-Malik” show about the word din?","o":["It only means prayer","It covers law and a whole way of life","It is only for Muslims"],"a":1,"w":"Allah used it for a king’s legal system."},{"q":"What does “above every possessor of knowledge is one more knowing” point to?","o":["Knowledge is useless","Ultimately, Allah is the All-Knowing","Only kings are wise"],"a":1,"w":"Allah raises ranks through faith and knowledge."}]},
{"id":18,"verses":[77,79],"title":"Restraint and Precise Speech","short":"Verses 77–79: “Allah forbid that we take anyone else”","big":"Shocked, the brothers turn on Binyamin and falsely accuse Yusuf himself: “a brother of his stole before.” Yusuf hides his outrage and stays silent. They beg him to take one of them instead, for the sake of their old father. Yusuf refuses with careful, truthful words: taking anyone but the one with whom the property was found would be unjust.","remember":["Having insisted on their innocence, the brothers now abandoned Binyamin.","They falsely accused Yusuf to his face, and he kept it within himself.","Self-defence is permissible but not always obligatory: sometimes silence is more beneficial.","“We see you as one of the muhsinin”: the same words his fellow prisoners used (12:36).","“The one with whom we found our property”, not “the thief”: Binyamin had not stolen.","Never lie. Tawriyah, true words the listener may take differently, is permissible."],"more":[{"t":"Abandoning Binyamin","l":["“If he has stolen, a brother of his stole before”: they distanced themselves by pointing to his full brother.","Scholars say this was a lie or an exaggerated story, told about Yusuf to his face."]},{"t":"Silence over self-defence","l":["Yusuf suppressed his outrage and did not reveal who he was.","In Part Three he defended himself against the charge of indecency. Here he stayed silent. Defending yourself is permissible, not always obligatory: choose your battles.","“You are in a worse position”, said within himself: you accuse Binyamin of theft, but you threw your brother into a well and sold him into slavery."]},{"t":"“He has a very old father”","l":["They panicked: they had sworn an oath to their father, and Binyamin now faced slavery.","Ya ayyuha al-‘Aziz: some say Yusuf now held his old master’s post; others say it is simply “Your Excellency”.","“He has a father”, not “we have”: for extra sympathy, and perhaps because they lacked the love Yusuf and Binyamin had for him.","Ab, father; shaykh, elder, man of knowledge, old man; kabir, great and honoured. All applied to Ya‘qub. Then they offered a swap: take one of us instead."]},{"t":"Precise, honest words","l":["Ma‘adha-llah again, as when the wife of al-‘Aziz tried to seduce him (12:23).","He did not say “the one who stole”, because Binyamin did not steal. He said “the one with whom we found our property”: neutral and true.","Lying is never allowed; the Prophet ﷺ never told a lie. Tawriyah is permissible: the brothers heard “theft”; Yusuf meant only where it was found.","“Otherwise we would be unjust”: punishing someone for another’s crime is injustice."]}],"home":"This week, when you are tempted to exaggerate or bend the truth, stop and find words that are fully true. And once, when someone wrongs you over something small, choose silence over a comeback.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Words from verses 77–79","help":"Tap a word on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["“Kept it within himself”","He hid his outrage and his identity"],["“You are in a worse position”","You threw your own brother into a well"],["Shaykh","An elder, a man of knowledge"],["Kabir","Great, noble and honoured"],["Ma‘adha-llah","The One whose refuge is sought is Allah"],["Tawriyah","True words the listener may take differently"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What did the brothers say when the cup was found?","o":["“Binyamin is innocent”","“A brother of his stole before”","“Take us all”"],"a":1,"w":"They distanced themselves from Binyamin."},{"q":"How did Yusuf react to being falsely accused?","o":["He revealed himself in anger","He kept it within himself","He punished them"],"a":1,"w":"Silence can be wiser than self-defence."},{"q":"Why did the brothers say “he has a father” rather than “we have”?","o":["For extra sympathy, and perhaps a lack of love","It is a mistake","They had a different father"],"a":0,"w":"Two reasons are given."},{"q":"Who else had called Yusuf “one of the muhsinin”?","o":["The King","His fellow prisoners","Ya‘qub"],"a":1,"w":"Noble character runs through the whole story."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf say “the one with whom we found our property”?","o":["Binyamin had not stolen, and he would not lie","He forgot Binyamin’s name","It was the legal formula in Egypt"],"a":0,"w":"Precise, truthful wording."},{"q":"What is tawriyah?","o":["A kind of lie","True words that the listener may understand differently","A legal penalty"],"a":1,"w":"Permissible when needed; lying never is."}]},
/* ---------- Part 9: Deprived of Three Sons ---------- */
{"id":19,"verses":[80,83],"title":"The Eldest’s Resolve","short":"Verses 80–83: remorse, and a truth no one believes","big":"Having failed to free Binyamin, the brothers confer in private. The eldest, full of remorse, refuses to leave Egypt and face his father. The rest go home with the truth, but after the lie about Yusuf, their father cannot believe them. Ya‘qub answers again with beautiful patience, and with hope that Allah will return all three sons.","remember":["Istay’asu: the added letters show they tried hard and only then gave up hope.","The Qur’an hides faults and names virtues: “one of them” in 12:10, “the eldest” in 12:80.","The eldest chose exile rather than break his oath and face his father in disgrace.","The boy who cried wolf: their truth was held hostage to their past.","“Bal” again, “your souls have tempted you” again, and beautiful patience again.","‘Asa, “perhaps”: “I am as My servant thinks of Me” (Bukhari 7405). Think well of Allah."],"more":[{"t":"Conferring in secret","l":["Khalasu: they broke away. Najiyya: in secret. They huddled together.","The eldest was full of remorse. Farrattum, “you fell short”: we failed regarding Yusuf.","Some scholars say he was the same brother who said “Don’t kill Yusuf; throw him into the well” (12:10)."]},{"t":"Hiding faults, naming virtues","l":["His blameworthy deed was left anonymous: “one of them said”. His praiseworthy deed is named: “the eldest said”.","The Prophet ﷺ did the same: for wrongdoing he said “why do some people do such-and-such?”, with no names; good deeds he praised by name."]},{"t":"Self-imposed exile","l":["“I will not leave this land until my father permits me, or Allah judges for me.”","Living as a foreigner then risked discrimination or prison, cut off from family. He preferred it to facing his father in disgrace.","“Allah judges for me”: by revelation to Ya‘qub, or by a calamity such as death or sickness.","“He is the Best of Judges”: he saw redemption in sincere repentance. These were the first stirrings of change among the brothers."]},{"t":"Truth held hostage","l":["“We testify only to what we know”: we saw the cup found, or we only told the minister your own law. “We could not guard against the unseen”: a legitimate excuse.","“Ask the town and the caravan”: supporting witnesses. They knew Binyamin was no thief, but his silence looked like guilt.","They had literally cried wolf about Yusuf decades earlier. Now, telling the truth, they were not believed. Lies today make your truth unbelievable tomorrow."]},{"t":"Strong faith in a compounded tragedy","l":["Ya‘qub knew Binyamin would never steal. Without clear evidence, he used the same words as over the shirt: “your souls have tempted you.”","‘Asa: he hoped Allah would return all three: Yusuf, Binyamin and the eldest.","Weak faith complains at the first trial, “Why me?”, and with more trials may question religion altogether, as Iblis questioned Allah’s command. Strong faith draws closer to Allah, trusting that He is All-Knowing, All-Wise."]}],"home":"This week, when you need to mention someone’s mistake, leave their name out. When you mention someone’s good deed, name them.","game":{"type":"order","title":"After the cup","help":"Tap the events in the order they come in the surah.","items":["They lose hope of freeing Binyamin","They confer in private","The eldest reminds them of their oath","The eldest stays behind in Egypt","They tell their father: your son stole","They say: ask the town and the caravan","Ya‘qub answers: beautiful patience"]},"quiz":[{"q":"What do the added letters in istay’asu show?","o":["They gave up at once","They tried persistently before losing hope","They were angry"],"a":1,"w":"The eloquence of Arabic (12:2)."},{"q":"Why is the eldest named in 12:80 but not in 12:10?","o":["He was absent in 12:10","Faults are concealed, virtues are named","It is a coincidence"],"a":1,"w":"The way of the Qur’an and the Sunnah."},{"q":"Why did the eldest stay in Egypt?","o":["To work for the minister","Out of remorse, rather than break his oath and face his father","He was arrested"],"a":1,"w":"Self-imposed exile."},{"q":"Why didn’t Ya‘qub believe the brothers, though they spoke the truth?","o":["They had lied about Yusuf before","They had no witnesses","He could not hear them"],"a":0,"w":"Their truth was held hostage to their past."},{"q":"Who did Ya‘qub hope Allah would bring back?","o":["Only Yusuf","Yusuf, Binyamin and the eldest","Only Binyamin"],"a":1,"w":"‘Asa: hope, not despair."},{"q":"“I am as My servant thinks of Me” teaches us to…","o":["Think well of Allah","Expect the worst","Rely on ourselves"],"a":0,"w":"Bukhari 7405."}]},
{"id":20,"verses":[84,87],"title":"Ya Asafa, and the Mercy of Allah","short":"Verses 84–87: grief, patience and hope","big":"Ya‘qub turns away, crying “O my sorrow over Yusuf!” His eyes turn white with grief, yet he holds it in. His sons say he will waste away; he answers that he complains only to Allah, and knows from Allah what they do not. Then he sends them to search, and forbids despair of Allah’s mercy.","remember":["Yusuf embodied all his pain: twenty-five years on, it had not faded.","Kazim, the intensive form: he had long been holding his grief in, and Allah praised him for it.","Crying is not impatience: “The eyes shed tears and the heart grieves, but we say only what pleases our Lord” (Bukhari 1303).","Complain to Allah, never about Allah.","“If you ask, ask Allah; if you seek help, seek it from Allah” (Tirmidhi 2516).","Despair of Allah’s mercy is a trait of disbelief: keep searching and striving."],"more":[{"t":"“O my sorrow over Yusuf!”","l":["He loved all his children, but Yusuf embodied all his pain.","Ya asafa: the vocative, the word for grief, and a final alif that calls up its image. Pain from the depths of the heart reaching the lips, impossible to render in English.","The brothers’ plan backfired: removing Yusuf only increased his father’s yearning. Shaytan deludes us into thinking sin will benefit us."]},{"t":"Eyes turned white","l":["Some say he went blind. The author leans to his eyes becoming glazed and white and his sight weakening. Allah knows best.","Kazim, from kazama, to hold back: the intensive form means he had long been suppressing it, as al-Sami‘, the All-Hearing, is more than sami‘, one who hears."]},{"t":"Turning only to Allah","l":["Perfect faith seeks sympathy from Allah alone. Turning to people is not a sin, but all help comes from Allah.","“Be mindful of Allah and He will protect you… If the nations gathered to benefit or harm you, they could only do what Allah has written. The pens are lifted and the pages are dry” (Tirmidhi 2516).","Levels: Ya‘qub, at ihsan, did not ask for a shoulder to cry on. Seeking comfort is allowed. A high practice is not to ask, but to accept comfort when offered, as the Prophet ﷺ accepted Khadijah’s."]},{"t":"Crying is not impatience","l":["Ya‘qub had beautiful patience and wept until his eyes turned white.","The Prophet ﷺ wept at the death of his son Ibrahim, and the Companions were surprised: “You too cry, O Messenger of Allah?”","Al-Hasan al-Basri wept at his son’s death. When a student frowned, he said: “Ya‘qub cried, and Allah did not rebuke him for it.”"]},{"t":"Complaining to Allah, and never despairing","l":["“You will not stop remembering Yusuf until you waste away (harad)”: divine irony. They wanted his love; they got his endless grief for Yusuf.","Bathth is the most severe grief, from “to scatter”. “I only complain of my anguish and sorrow to Allah”: innama, to Him alone.","Complaining to Allah, as the Prophet ﷺ did after Ta’if, is a sign of iman. Complaining about Allah, “Why are You doing this to me?”, is prohibited.","“I know from Allah what you do not know”: he knew Yusuf’s dream, so Yusuf had to be alive.","Tahassasu: search with all your senses. Rawh, Allah’s mercy and relief, shares a root with ruh, the soul: rawh comforts, ruh gives life."]}],"home":"Tonight, tell Allah plainly about something that is hurting you, and ask for His help. Then take one practical step towards fixing it, without giving up hope.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"To Allah, or about Allah?","help":"Is each complaining to Allah (good), or about Allah (prohibited)?","buckets":["Complaining to Allah","Complaining about Allah"],"items":[["Telling Allah your difficulties",0],["Asking for His compassion",0],["What the Prophet ﷺ did after Ta’if",0],["“Why are You doing this to me?”",1],["“It’s not fair!”",1],["Questioning Allah’s wisdom",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Ya‘qub grieve for Yusuf when Binyamin and the eldest were also gone?","o":["He did not love the others","Yusuf embodied all his pain","He had forgotten the others"],"a":1,"w":"He loved all his children."},{"q":"What does kazim tell us about Ya‘qub?","o":["He shouted his grief","He had long been holding his grief in","He felt nothing"],"a":1,"w":"Allah praised him for controlling his emotions."},{"q":"Is crying a lack of patience?","o":["Yes, always","No: Ya‘qub and the Prophet ﷺ both wept","Only for men"],"a":1,"w":"“The eyes shed tears and the heart grieves.”"},{"q":"Which is prohibited?","o":["Complaining to Allah","Complaining about Allah","Making du‘a"],"a":1,"w":"Complaining to Allah is a sign of iman."},{"q":"How did Ya‘qub know Yusuf was alive?","o":["A letter","Yusuf’s dream had not yet come true","The brothers told him"],"a":1,"w":"“I know from Allah what you do not know.”"},{"q":"What did Ya‘qub say about despairing of Allah’s mercy?","o":["It is understandable","Only the disbelieving people despair","It is permitted after many years"],"a":1,"w":"Keep searching, never despair."}]},
/* ---------- Part 10: Yusuf Reveals His Identity ---------- */
{"id":21,"verses":[88,90],"title":"“Are You Indeed Yusuf?”","short":"Verses 88–90: the reveal","big":"On their third visit, impoverished by the famine, the brothers beg for charity. Yusuf asks: “Do you know what you did to Yusuf and his brother when you were ignorant?” Stunned, they ask, “Are you indeed Yusuf?” He answers, “I am Yusuf, and this is my brother. Allah has been gracious to us”: whoever is mindful of Allah and patient, Allah never wastes their reward.","remember":["Massana al-durr, bida‘ah muzjah: hardship had touched them, and they brought poor goods.","“Allah rewards the charitable”: the irony was not lost on Yusuf.","He spoke of himself in the third person, and said “when you were ignorant”: a face-saving excuse.","A-innaka la-anta Yusuf: triple emphasis, “Are you really, really Yusuf?”","“Allah has been gracious to us”: manna, an undeserved gift. He attributed all good to Allah.","The surah in one sentence: whoever is mindful of Allah and patient will be rewarded."],"more":[{"t":"The third visit","l":["Nine brothers came, since the eldest had stayed in Egypt. Nearly seven years of drought had impoverished them.","Bida‘ah muzjah: second-rate goods. They were ashamed: “We are not beggars; we brought something, but it is poor.”","They asked for full measure, and more, as charity: “Allah rewards the charitable.” No one needed that reminder more than they did."]},{"t":"Grace in the question","l":["“Do you know what you did to Yusuf and his brother?” How could the Egyptian minister know their most closely guarded secret?","He spoke of “Yusuf” in the third person to lessen their guilt, and “when you were ignorant” gave them an excuse.","With full power and every justification for contempt, he did not criticise them directly: the perfection of his character."]},{"t":"Why now?","l":["Allah commanded it now: prophets wait for Allah’s command, as the Prophet ﷺ did not migrate until permitted.","A practical reason in early tafsir: the old King had died and a young son took the throne, so senior ministers gained power and Yusuf could bring his family."]},{"t":"“I am Yusuf, and this is my brother”","l":["He called Binyamin into the court.","“Allah has been gracious to us”: none of this is my power; I am not gloating. Manna is a gift given purely out of Allah’s generosity.","Humility in grammar: “whoever is mindful and patient”, not “I was righteous and patient”."]},{"t":"Sabr and taqwa","l":["Sabr carried him through the well, slavery and prison to the ministry. Sabr and taqwa are the two pillars of the whole story.","A message to the brothers too: you tried to get what you wanted by disobeying Allah, and did not get it; I was patient and trusted Him, and He gave me beyond imagination.","Reformation, not revenge, is the goal of the righteous."]}],"home":"The next time something good happens to you, say “Allah has been gracious to me” before mentioning anything you did.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Words from verses 88–90","help":"Tap a word on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Massana al-durr","Severe hardship has touched us"],["Bida‘ah muzjah","Poor-quality goods"],["“When you were ignorant”","A face-saving excuse"],["A-innaka la-anta Yusuf","“Are you really, really Yusuf?”"],["Manna","An undeserved gift from Allah"],["Sabr and taqwa","The two pillars of the story"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"How many brothers came on the third visit?","o":["Eleven","Ten","Nine"],"a":2,"w":"The eldest had stayed in Egypt."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf speak of “Yusuf” in the third person?","o":["To hide who he was forever","To lessen their guilt","He forgot to say “me”"],"a":1,"w":"Grace in the question."},{"q":"What did “when you were ignorant” give the brothers?","o":["A face-saving excuse","A punishment","A riddle"],"a":0,"w":"He did not criticise them directly."},{"q":"Who did Yusuf credit for his success?","o":["Himself","The King","Allah: “Allah has been gracious to us”"],"a":2,"w":"Manna: an undeserved gift."},{"q":"What is the surah’s message in one sentence?","o":["The strong always win","Whoever is mindful of Allah and patient will be rewarded","Never trust family"],"a":1,"w":"Taqwa and sabr."},{"q":"What is the goal of the righteous towards those who wronged them?","o":["Revenge","Reformation","Avoidance forever"],"a":1,"w":"Not revenge."}]},
{"id":22,"verses":[91,93],"title":"La Tathriba ‘Alaykum al-Yawm","short":"Verses 91–93: “No blame on you today”","big":"The brothers confess: “Allah has preferred you over us, and we were indeed sinners.” Yusuf forgives them at once, without a word of reproach: “No blame on you today. Allah will forgive you.” The Prophet ﷺ would one day say the same words to Quraysh. Then Yusuf sends his shirt to restore his father’s sight, and invites the whole family to Egypt.","remember":["Their confession was the first step towards repentance.","Tathrib: reproaching someone to make them feel guilty. Yusuf took himself out of the picture entirely.","Wrongs against people need the victim’s forgiveness: be very careful not to oppress anyone.","Allah’s forgiveness is easier to gain than people’s, because He is the Most Merciful of the merciful.","At the Conquest of Makkah the Prophet ﷺ said: “I say to you what Yusuf said to his brothers.”","Three shirts: false blood, torn from the back, and the shirt that heals."],"more":[{"t":"The confession","l":["Yusuf’s words had their effect. “Tallahi” is an oath, and “wa in” adds emphasis: “Allah has preferred you over us, and we were indeed sinners.”","Admitting guilt is the first step towards repentance."]},{"t":"“No blame on you today”","l":["La tathriba ‘alaykum al-yawm: no reproach, no reminding them of their sins to make them feel guilty.","He did not even say “I won’t blame you”. He made it generic and neutral, removing himself from the picture."]},{"t":"How could he say “Allah will forgive you”?","l":["The wrong was done to Yusuf, and he forgave, so Allah will forgive. Crimes against people need the victim’s forgiveness, or Allah settles them on the Day of Judgement, even among animals.","Or it means “I hope Allah will forgive you.” Both answers are valid.","Allah may forgive sins against Himself, but not wrongs against people unless the victim forgives."]},{"t":"The Prophet ﷺ lived this surah","l":["Surah Yusuf was revealed in the Year of Sorrow: Abu Talib and Khadijah died, and Ta’if rejected him violently. It came to lift his spirits.","At the Conquest of Makkah he asked Quraysh how he should treat them. They said: “We expect nothing but good from you.”","He said: “I say to you what Yusuf said to his brothers: ‘No blame on you today.’ Go, you are free” (Ibn Sa‘d, al-Tabaqat al-Kabir)."]},{"t":"The shirt’s three appearances","l":["1. Stained with false blood (12:18): evidence of a lie, and the start of Ya‘qub’s grief.","2. Torn from the back (12:25–28): evidence of innocence, saving Yusuf’s honour.","3. Sent to Ya‘qub (12:93): a healer that restores his sight. Its fragrance tells him Yusuf is alive before it even arrives.","“Bring me your whole family”: having pardoned them, Yusuf invites them all to live in comfort in Egypt."]}],"home":"Think of someone who has wronged you and has said sorry, or would like to. Forgive them this week, and do not remind them of what they did.","game":{"type":"order","title":"From the false shirt to forgiveness","help":"Tap the events in the order they come in the surah.","items":["The shirt with false blood","The shirt torn from the back","The brothers admit they were sinners","Yusuf: “No blame on you today”","Yusuf: “Allah will forgive you”","Yusuf sends his shirt to his father","“Bring me your whole family”"]},"quiz":[{"q":"What was the brothers’ first step towards repentance?","o":["Running away","Admitting they were sinners","Paying Yusuf back"],"a":1,"w":"“We were indeed sinners.”"},{"q":"What is tathrib?","o":["Reproaching someone to make them feel guilty","A kind of oath","A gift of food"],"a":0,"w":"Yusuf refused to do it."},{"q":"Why should we be very careful not to wrong people?","o":["Allah forgives those wrongs automatically","Wrongs against people need the victim’s forgiveness","People forget quickly"],"a":1,"w":"Or Allah settles them on the Day of Judgement."},{"q":"When did the Prophet ﷺ repeat Yusuf’s words?","o":["At the Battle of Badr","At the Conquest of Makkah","At Ta’if"],"a":1,"w":"“No blame on you today. Go, you are free.”"},{"q":"In which year was Surah Yusuf revealed?","o":["The Year of the Elephant","The Year of Sorrow","The year of the Hijrah"],"a":1,"w":"To lift the Prophet’s ﷺ spirits."},{"q":"What does the third shirt do?","o":["Proves a lie","Saves Yusuf’s honour","Restores Ya‘qub’s sight"],"a":2,"w":"Its fragrance arrives first."}]},
/* ---------- Part 11: The Dream Fulfilled ---------- */
{"id":23,"verses":[94,98],"title":"The Scent, the Sight, the Seeking of Forgiveness","short":"Verses 94–98: “Did I not tell you?”","big":"As the caravan leaves Egypt with Yusuf’s shirt, Ya‘qub senses his son’s scent from hundreds of miles away. His family calls it an old delusion, but the bearer of good news casts the shirt over his face and his sight returns. His sons ask him to seek forgiveness for them, and he promises to, at the most blessed time.","remember":["Ya‘qub sensed Yusuf’s scent from far away: firasah, and a sign from Allah.","Many say the bearer of good news was the same son who brought the bloodied shirt decades before.","Allah names the good and passes over the bad, all through the surah and the Qur’an.","“Did I not tell you?”: through all those years he never lost hope, and Allah vindicated him.","Admitting guilt is the first step of repentance: “Regret is repentance” (Ibn Majah 4252).","Husn al-khatimah: a person’s final state depends on how they end, not how they began."],"more":[{"t":"The scent across the miles","l":["The caravan left Egypt with Yusuf’s shirt among the grain sacks, yet Ya‘qub sensed his son was near.","He expected to be called senile, but in his heart he knew. Some scholars say it was his grandchildren who answered so harshly: “you are still in your old delusion.” They were wrong."]},{"t":"The bearer of good news","l":["Al-bashir: imagine the feelings of the son who once caused his father’s lasting grief, now carrying the shirt that ends it.","Allah names the bearer of glad tidings and passes over the bearer of the false shirt.","With the touch of the shirt, Ya‘qub could see again: “Did I not tell you that I know from Allah what you do not know?”"]},{"t":"“I will ask my Lord to forgive you”","l":["They had already asked Yusuf; now they asked their father. They were sincerely repentant.","Sawfa, “I will”, means later. Most scholars say he waited for the last third of the night, to ask in sujud at the most blessed time.","“Our Lord descends every night to the lowest heaven when the last third remains and says: Who will call on Me, that I may answer? Who will ask, that I may give? Who will seek forgiveness, that I may forgive?” (Bukhari 1145)."]},{"t":"A good ending","l":["A modern court would charge the brothers with the attempted murder of a child, yet Allah gave them the chance to repent.","The majority view is that they were forgiven and became prophets, which is why the dream showed them as stars.","The gates of mercy are open, but repent without delay: repentance is not accepted in the throes of death. If Allah forgave them, there is surely hope for us."]}],"home":"Set an alarm for the last third of the night once this week. Pray two rak‘ahs and ask Allah’s forgiveness for something specific.","game":{"type":"order","title":"From the scent to forgiveness","help":"Tap the events in the order they come in the surah.","items":["The caravan leaves Egypt with the shirt","Ya‘qub: “I sense the scent of Yusuf”","They say: “You are still in your old delusion”","The bearer of good news casts the shirt over his face","Ya‘qub sees again","His sons ask him to seek forgiveness for them","Ya‘qub: “I will ask my Lord to forgive you”"]},"quiz":[{"q":"How did Ya‘qub know Yusuf was near?","o":["A messenger told him","He sensed his scent: firasah, a sign from Allah","He saw the caravan"],"a":1,"w":"From hundreds of miles away."},{"q":"Who do many scholars say brought the healing shirt?","o":["Binyamin","The same son who brought the bloodied shirt","A servant of Yusuf"],"a":1,"w":"The one who caused the grief carried the shirt that ended it."},{"q":"What does the Qur’an do with the good and the bad in this scene?","o":["Names the good and passes over the bad","Names both equally","Mentions only the bad"],"a":0,"w":"A pattern through the whole Qur’an."},{"q":"Why did Ya‘qub say “I will ask” rather than ask at once?","o":["He was still angry","He waited for the last third of the night","He forgot"],"a":1,"w":"The most blessed time for du‘a."},{"q":"What is the first step of repentance?","o":["Giving charity","Admitting guilt","Fasting"],"a":1,"w":"“Regret is repentance.”"},{"q":"What does husn al-khatimah teach?","o":["How you begin matters most","How you end matters most","Neither matters"],"a":1,"w":"Even grave sinners can end well."}]},
{"id":24,"verses":[99,100],"title":"Ta’wil Ru’yaya: The Dream Realised","short":"Verses 99–100: “My Lord has made it true”","big":"The whole family arrives in Egypt as emigrants. Yusuf welcomes them, raises his parents onto the throne, and they bow before him: the dream of eleven stars, the sun and the moon has come true. Yusuf thanks Allah, leaves out the well to spare his brothers, and blames Shaytan, not them.","remember":["About seventy people came as emigrants, not travellers.","“Enter Egypt, Allah willing, in safety”: Allah is the source of safety; Yusuf is only a means.","Bowing to people as respect was allowed in Ya‘qub’s law; in ours, bowing and prostration are for Allah alone.","A maternal aunt (khalah) is like a mother.","Yusuf mentioned the prison but not the well, to spare his brothers embarrassment.","Al-Latif: aware of the most hidden matters, and kind to us despite our faults."],"more":[{"t":"The fourth journey","l":["Parents, wives and children, with all their possessions. Five centuries later, the Children of Isra’il would leave Egypt for Palestine under Musa.","Scholars say Yusuf led a procession to the city’s outskirts to greet them.","It was Ya‘qub’s first time in Egypt. He had lived as a Bedouin in a barren valley until the drought; now, old, he looked forward to peace with his beloved son."]},{"t":"Prostration: then and now","l":["“Kharru lahu sujjada”: they bowed before him. Sujud can mean falling on the face or bowing; scholars differ.","In Ya‘qub’s shari‘ah, lowering the head as respect was permitted. In ours it is forbidden: bowing and prostration are for Allah alone.","Mu‘adh prostrated to the Prophet ﷺ after seeing people in Syria prostrate to their bishops. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not do that!” (Ibn Majah 1853)."]},{"t":"“His parents”","l":["Yusuf’s mother had died long before; Ya‘qub’s wife was Yusuf’s maternal aunt.","The Prophet ﷺ confirmed that a khalah has the rights of a mother and may be called “mother”."]},{"t":"Gratitude with grace","l":["Ta’wil here means the dream has become real. Prophets’ dreams are revelation.","“He was kind to me when He brought me out of prison”: he credits Allah immediately.","Being saved from the well was the bigger blessing, yet he left it out to spare his brothers, and blamed Shaytan: “after Shaytan sowed discord between me and my brothers.”","Al-Latif, al-‘Alim, al-Hakim: Allah knew all along. No one in the family knew what awaited them."]}],"home":"Next time you tell the story of a hard time in your life, leave out other people’s faults, and mention Allah’s kindness first.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"What Yusuf said, and what he left out","help":"Did Yusuf mention it in verse 100, or leave it out?","buckets":["He mentioned it","He left it out"],"items":[["Allah bringing him out of prison",0],["Allah bringing the family from the desert",0],["Shaytan sowing discord",0],["Allah, the Subtle, the All-Knowing",0],["Being saved from the well",1],["His brothers’ part in it",1],["His own effort and cleverness",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"How did the family come to Egypt this time?","o":["As traders","As emigrants, about seventy people","As prisoners"],"a":1,"w":"The fourth journey."},{"q":"What does “Allah willing, in safety” remind them?","o":["Egypt has strong walls","Allah is the source of safety; Yusuf is only a means","The King protects them"],"a":1,"w":"Credit belongs to Allah."},{"q":"Is bowing to people permitted in our shari‘ah?","o":["Yes, as respect","No: bowing and prostration are for Allah alone","Only to parents"],"a":1,"w":"“Do not do that!” (Ibn Majah 1853)."},{"q":"Why did Yusuf call his aunt one of his “parents”?","o":["She adopted him formally","A maternal aunt has the rights of a mother","It is a translation error"],"a":1,"w":"Confirmed by the Prophet ﷺ."},{"q":"Why didn’t Yusuf mention the well?","o":["He had forgotten it","To spare his brothers embarrassment","It was not important"],"a":1,"w":"He blamed Shaytan, not them."},{"q":"What does al-Latif mean?","o":["The Strong","Aware of hidden matters, and kind","The King"],"a":1,"w":"Primary and secondary meanings."}]},
{"id":25,"verses":[101,101],"title":"Tawaffani Musliman","short":"Verse 101: Yusuf’s du‘a","big":"At the height of his success, Yusuf turns to his Lord: he praises Him for the authority and knowledge He gave, calls Him Originator of the heavens and earth and his Protector in both worlds, and asks for the most precious thing of all: to die as a Muslim and be joined with the righteous.","remember":["Ar-Rabb: Owner, Nourisher and Lord, all on display in this du‘a.","“Something of” authority: humility, since a minister’s power is tiny next to Allah’s.","Begin du‘a with praise and thanks: “If you are grateful, I will increase you” (14:7).","Fatir: only Allah creates from nothing. Waliyy: the one close to you, Protector and friend.","“Let me die as a Muslim”: after every blessing, he asks for the most precious one.","“A man is upon the religion of his close friend, so look at whom you befriend” (Tirmidhi 2378)."],"more":[{"t":"“Something of authority”","l":["Min al-mulk alludes to al-Malik, the Owner of all dominion, and al-‘Alim. His power and his knowledge of dreams were a portion from Allah.","Allah usually chooses prophets for spirituality and knowledge, not worldly power. Yusuf also had authority; Sulayman had an unmatched kingdom; the Prophet ﷺ combined political authority as head of state with prophetic knowledge."]},{"t":"Praise first","l":["Mentioning Allah’s favours is gratitude, and gratitude brings more (14:7).","It acknowledges Allah as al-Akram, the Most Generous. The best way to ask is to begin with thanks and praise."]},{"t":"Fatir and Waliyy","l":["Fatir al-samawati wa-l-ard: only Allah creates from nothing; humans only modify what He made.","Al-Waliyy, from waliya, to be next to: the close one, protector and friend. Allah is the walī of the believers and of orphans.","Together: Creator of our physical being and Guardian of our spiritual being. “You created all of this, protected me throughout, and had a plan.”"]},{"t":"The two requests","l":["“Let me die as a Muslim”: what matters in the end is meeting Allah in submission.","“Join me with the righteous”: to be raised in the best company. Choose your friends wisely.","The Prophet’s ﷺ final words, narrated by ‘A’ishah: “…with those You have blessed… join me with the Highest Companion,” echoing 4:69."]},{"t":"A perfect ending","l":["Historically, Ya‘qub died two or three years after moving to Egypt, and Yusuf, grief-stricken, died not long after.","The most righteous person he knew was his father, which makes “join me with the righteous” even more moving.","In a few words Yusuf affirms tawhid, the perfection of Allah’s Names, and sincere submission."]}],"home":"Memorise “tawaffani musliman wa alhiqni bi-l-salihin” and add it to your du‘a after each prayer this week, after first praising Allah.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Words of Yusuf’s du‘a","help":"Tap a word on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Rabbi","My Owner, Nourisher and Lord"],["Min al-mulk","“Something of” authority"],["Fatir","The Originator, who creates from nothing"],["Waliyy","The one close to me: Protector and friend"],["Tawaffani musliman","Let me die as a Muslim"],["Alhiqni bi-l-salihin","Join me with the righteous"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Yusuf say “something of” authority?","o":["He had very little power","Humility: his power was tiny next to Allah’s","He had lost his post"],"a":1,"w":"Min al-mulk alludes to al-Malik."},{"q":"How should du‘a begin, according to Yusuf’s example?","o":["With the request","With praise and thanks","With complaints"],"a":1,"w":"Gratitude brings increase (14:7)."},{"q":"What does Fatir mean?","o":["The One who creates from nothing","The Merciful","The Judge"],"a":0,"w":"Humans only modify what He made."},{"q":"What did Yusuf ask for after listing his blessings?","o":["More wealth","To die as a Muslim and join the righteous","A longer life"],"a":1,"w":"The most precious thing."},{"q":"What does “a man is upon the religion of his close friend” teach?","o":["Friends do not matter","Choose your friends carefully","Have many friends"],"a":1,"w":"Tirmidhi 2378."},{"q":"Whose final words echoed this du‘a?","o":["Ya‘qub’s","The Prophet’s ﷺ: “the Highest Companion”","The King’s"],"a":1,"w":"Narrated by ‘A’ishah."}]},
/* ---------- Part 12: Reminders to the Prophet ﷺ ---------- */
{"id":26,"verses":[102,104],"title":"News of the Unseen","short":"Verses 102–104: revelation, eagerness and sincerity","big":"The story ends, and Allah turns to the Prophet ﷺ: this is news of the unseen, revealed to you; you were not there when they plotted. Most people will not believe, however eager you are. And you ask them for no reward: it is only a reminder for all the worlds.","remember":["Dhalika, “that”, raises the story above all others.","Allah chose the most secret scene, the plotting, which only He could know.","Makkah had no books and no Jewish or Christian community; the first Arabic Bible came around 180 AH.","Most people will not believe: do your best; results belong to Allah. Quality over quantity.","Prophets seek no worldly reward; even charity was forbidden to the Prophet ﷺ and his family.","Dhikr: the Qur’an reminds us of what our fitrah already knows."],"more":[{"t":"Proof of revelation","l":["Allah could have said “you were not at the well” or “in the palace”. He chose the plotting, which no one but Allah could know.","Makkah had about a thousand people, perhaps ten of them literate, with no books and no scriptural communities.","“He copied the Bible”? The first Arabic translation appeared generations later, versions differ widely, and many details of Yusuf’s story are only in the Qur’an. The only logical answer is revelation."]},{"t":"A realistic view of da‘wah","l":["Harasta: keen, zealous, anxious for people, a defining quality of the Prophet ﷺ (9:128).","Naive da‘wah assumes a few proofs will convince everyone. The Prophet ﷺ preached for thirteen years in Makkah, and most rejected him though they knew his nobility.","Those who believed were few and mostly poor; the rich and powerful were often last."]},{"t":"Proof of sincerity","l":["Why would one of their own endure persecution? Not for money, power or prestige. “I ask you for no wage” is the Sunnah of all prophets.","‘Umar found him on a palm-leaf mat that had marked his side, and wept: “Caesar and Khosrau live in luxury!” He said: “Are you not content that they have this world and we the Hereafter?” (Bukhari 4913)."]},{"t":"A reminder for all the worlds","l":["Al-‘alamin: humans and jinn, nations, every category of being.","Names of the Qur’an: Qur’an, the recited; Kitab, the written; al-Furqan, the criterion; Dhikr, the reminder.","Dhikr: it reminds us of what our fitrah knows; it is a continuous reminder; and it is an honour (43:44). Within decades of receiving it, a divided people became a civilisation that toppled Persia."]}],"home":"Pick one person you would like to see closer to Allah. Make du‘a for them daily this week, and leave the result to Allah.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Revelation and sincerity","help":"Is each a proof that the story is revelation, or a proof of the Prophet’s ﷺ sincerity?","buckets":["Proof of revelation","Proof of sincerity"],"items":[["Makkah had no books",0],["No Jewish or Christian community to learn from",0],["The first Arabic Bible came about 180 AH",0],["Details found only in the Qur’an",0],["He asked for no wage",1],["Charity was forbidden to him and his family",1],["He slept on a palm-leaf mat",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Allah mention the plotting scene in verse 102?","o":["It was the longest scene","It was the most secret, known only to Allah","It happened in Makkah"],"a":1,"w":"No one but Allah could know it."},{"q":"When did the first Arabic Bible translation appear?","o":["Before the Prophet ﷺ","Around 180 AH","During the Hijrah"],"a":1,"w":"Generations after the Prophet ﷺ."},{"q":"What does verse 103 teach about da‘wah?","o":["Everyone will believe with enough proof","Most will not believe; do your best and leave results to Allah","Stop calling people"],"a":1,"w":"Quality over quantity."},{"q":"Why was charity forbidden to the Prophet ﷺ and his family?","o":["They were wealthy","So no one could doubt his sincerity","It was a punishment"],"a":1,"w":"No worldly gain."},{"q":"What did the Prophet ﷺ tell ‘Umar about Caesar and Khosrau?","o":["“We should live like them”","“They have this world, and we the Hereafter”","“They will become Muslim”"],"a":1,"w":"Bukhari 4913."},{"q":"What does the name Dhikr mean?","o":["The criterion","The reminder","The written"],"a":1,"w":"It reminds us of what our fitrah knows."}]},
{"id":27,"verses":[105,108],"title":"Signs, Shirk, and the Way of the Prophet ﷺ","short":"Verses 105–108: “This is my way”","big":"People walk past Allah’s signs without reflecting. Most who believe in Allah still associate partners with Him. Do they feel safe from a sudden punishment, or the Hour? Then the Prophet ﷺ is told to declare his way: one path, followed with clear insight, calling to Allah, with no shirk.","remember":["Yamurrun: they walk right past the signs without thinking.","Quraysh believed in Allah as Creator, yet were not Muslims: shirk cancels belief.","Modern idols: the worship of wealth, power, fame and celebrities.","Do not hide the Qur’an’s warnings: Hell is real.","Sabili, “my way”, is singular: truth is one, falsehood is many.","Da‘wah is on every Muslim, and good character is its first step."],"more":[{"t":"Signs everywhere","l":["In nature: the sun, moon, stars, oceans, mountains, and our own souls. In history: the ruins of ‘Ad and Thamud, carved into mountains.","Most of humanity sees Allah’s signs and does not stop to reflect."]},{"t":"Belief is not enough without tawhid","l":["Quraysh believed in Allah as Creator and All-Powerful, but gave idols a share of His power. Allah calls shirk a great injustice.","A reminder to Muslims: purify belief of all shirk, open or hidden, including idolising wealth, fame and celebrities as if they were gods."]},{"t":"Don’t hide the warnings","l":["Ghashiyah: that which covers and overwhelms. “Do they feel secure…?”","The Qur’an motivates with Paradise and mercy and also warns of punishment. Avoiding talk of Hell to seem polite is a mistake: the threat is no bluff."]},{"t":"One straight path","l":["The Prophet ﷺ drew one straight line, “This is Allah’s path,” then lines to either side: “On each is a devil calling to it,” and recited 6:153 (Ahmad 4423).","Allah brings people out of the darknesses, plural, into the light, singular (2:257).","‘Ala basirah: with clear insight rooted in knowledge. “I have left you on a clear path, its night like its day” (Ibn Majah 44)."]},{"t":"“I and whoever follows me”","l":["Every follower must call to Allah by the Prophet’s ﷺ method: da‘wah is fard ‘ayn on every adult, sane Muslim.","Da‘wah is not just debate. Throughout Yusuf’s story, good conduct came first and moved hearts.","“Glory be to Allah, and I am not of the polytheists.”"]}],"home":"Take a ten-minute walk this week with no phone, and name five signs of Allah you see. Then ask: is there anything I love so much it competes with Allah?","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Signs or idols?","help":"Is each a sign of Allah to reflect on, or a modern idol to beware of?","buckets":["A sign of Allah","A modern idol"],"items":[["The sun and the moon",0],["Our own souls",0],["The ruins of past nations",0],["Worshipping wealth",1],["Chasing fame",1],["Treating celebrities like gods",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What does yamurrun describe?","o":["Running to the mosque","Walking past Allah’s signs without thinking","Travelling for trade"],"a":1,"w":"The state of most of humanity."},{"q":"Why weren’t Quraysh Muslims, though they believed in Allah?","o":["They did not pray","They associated partners with Him","They were not Arabs"],"a":1,"w":"Shirk cancels belief."},{"q":"What does the singular “sabili” teach?","o":["There are many paths to Allah","The path to Allah is one","Paths do not matter"],"a":1,"w":"Truth is one; falsehood is many."},{"q":"What does ‘ala basirah mean?","o":["With clear insight rooted in knowledge","With a loud voice","With wealth"],"a":0,"w":"Following the Prophet ﷺ needs knowledge."},{"q":"Who is obliged to call to Allah?","o":["Only scholars","Every adult, sane Muslim","Only prophets"],"a":1,"w":"Da‘wah is fard ‘ayn."},{"q":"What is the first step of da‘wah in Yusuf’s story?","o":["Winning debates","Good character","Writing books"],"a":1,"w":"Conduct moves hearts."}]},
{"id":28,"verses":[109,111],"title":"‘Ibrah for People of Understanding","short":"Verses 109–111: the surah’s closing","big":"Allah consoles the Prophet ﷺ: the messengers before you were men from the towns, and they too were rejected, until, at the last moment, Our help came. The surah closes: in their stories is ‘ibrah, a deep lesson for people of understanding. It is no invented tale, but confirmation, explanation, guidance and mercy.","remember":["Past prophets lived in towns and suffered from their people: don’t be shocked by rejection.","Every civilisation thinks it is indestructible: drop the arrogance.","Two authentic readings, kudhibu and kudhdhibu, are both valid and complement each other.","Fa-nunji: a promise to the Prophet ﷺ that Allah will save him, fulfilled again and again.","‘Ibrah comes from ‘abara, to cross over: reflection carries us to wisdom.","Move from the ayat (12:7) to the ‘ibrah (12:111)."],"more":[{"t":"Consolation","l":["Past prophets also lived in towns and suffered from their people. It is human to find comfort in knowing others faced the same.","The standard Sunni position is that all prophets were men. Maryam was spoken to by an angel but received no wahy or book. This does not make women spiritually inferior: men and women are equal before Allah, with different roles.","“Have they not travelled the land?” The Arabs knew the fate of Thamud. Your job is only to do your best; the Home of the Hereafter is better."]},{"t":"Help at the last moment","l":["Kudhibu: the people thought the prophets’ promises were lies, and then punishment came and the believers were saved.","Kudhdhibu: the prophets realised their nations had rejected them and would never believe.","Fa-nujjiya, “were saved”, and fa-nunji, “We save”: a promise fulfilled in the Sirah, through foiled assassination attempts and at Badr, Uhud and al-Ahzab."]},{"t":"From ayat to ‘ibrah","l":["The opening: “We relate to you the best of stories” (12:3), and “signs for those who ask” (12:7).","The closing: “in their stories there was ‘ibrah for people of understanding” (12:111).","Listening with an open heart reveals signs; reflecting deeply reveals hidden wisdom."]},{"t":"Not a fabricated tale","l":["Knowing a story is true changes how we hear it. This is not a fairy tale.","It confirms earlier revelation, explains all things in detail, and is guidance and mercy for believers.","The author’s closing du‘a: may Allah grant us the tawakkul of Ya‘qub, the patience of Yusuf, and the repentance of his brothers, and make us people of the Qur’an. Amin."]}],"home":"Write down the one ‘ibrah from Surah Yusuf that has affected you most, and one change you will make because of it.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Words from verses 109–111","help":"Tap a word on the left, then its meaning on the right.","pairs":[["Kudhibu","The people thought the promises were lies"],["Kudhdhibu","The prophets realised they were rejected"],["Fa-nunji","A promise: “We will save you”"],["‘Ibrah","A lesson that carries you across to wisdom"],["Ayat (12:7)","Signs for those who ask"],["“Not a fabricated tale”","A true story, not a fairy tale"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"How does verse 109 console the Prophet ﷺ?","o":["Past prophets were also rejected by their people","His people will all believe","He will become a king"],"a":0,"w":"Don’t be shocked by rejection."},{"q":"What is the standard Sunni position on prophets?","o":["All were men","Some were angels","Maryam was a prophetess"],"a":0,"w":"Men and women remain spiritually equal before Allah."},{"q":"What do the two readings kudhibu and kudhdhibu show?","o":["One is wrong","Authentic readings are equally valid and complement each other","They mean the same thing"],"a":1,"w":"A principle of the qira’at."},{"q":"How was “We will save you” fulfilled for the Prophet ﷺ?","o":["He was never threatened","Foiled assassination attempts, and at Badr, Uhud and al-Ahzab","He left Arabia"],"a":1,"w":"Allah’s help came."},{"q":"What does ‘ibrah come from?","o":["To cross over","To write","To travel"],"a":0,"w":"Reflection carries us to wisdom."},{"q":"What does the surah’s closing du‘a ask for?","o":["Wealth, power and fame","The tawakkul of Ya‘qub, the patience of Yusuf and the repentance of his brothers","A long life"],"a":1,"w":"And to be people of the Qur’an."}]},
/* ---------- Part 13: Fifty Lessons from Surah Yusuf ---------- */
{"id":29,"verses":[[3,3],[6,6],[67,67],[101,101]],"title":"Fifty Lessons I: The Qur’an, Faith and Family","short":"Lessons on knowledge, trust in Allah, and the home","big":"“In their stories there was truly a lesson for people of reason” (12:111). One reading is not enough: each return to the surah yields new benefits. These lessons gather what it teaches about the Qur’an and knowledge, faith in Allah’s plan, and family life.","remember":["Lesson 3: when Allah wants good for someone, He gives them understanding of the religion (Bukhari 71).","Lesson 4: firasah is real, a fruit of piety. It carries no legal weight, but can guide life decisions.","Lesson 32: tawakkul means doing your very best while knowing the results are in Allah’s hands.","Lesson 8: fairness is not the same as love. Treat children fairly; love is beyond our control.","Lesson 15: to raise righteous children, first be righteous yourself.","Lesson 50: what matters is where you end, not where you began."],"more":[{"t":"The Qur’an and knowledge (1, 2, 3, 5)","l":["1. The miracle of the Qur’an’s language: riveting detail with nothing superfluous. Compare other scriptures’ versions to see the difference.","2. Its stories are the best: study them, tell them to your children, and let this surah be a gateway to the others.","3. The ultimate blessings are faith and knowledge, as Allah gave the family of Ishaq knowledge, wisdom and prophethood.","5. True dreams and their interpretation are gifts Allah grants to some of His most righteous servants: Yusuf’s, the prisoners’ and the King’s."]},{"t":"Faith, trust and Allah’s plan (4, 7, 32, 36)","l":["4. Firasah comes from closeness to Allah, and the closer you are, the more accurate it becomes.","7. Righteousness brings success in both lives: everyone attested to Yusuf’s character, yet his eyes stayed on the Hereafter.","32. Yusuf asked the freed prisoner to mention him: both the goal and the road to it are from Allah.","36. Ascribe good to Allah and evil to Shaytan: “He was kind to me… after Shaytan sowed discord” (12:100)."]},{"t":"Hope to the very end (49, 50)","l":["49. Every one of Yusuf’s situations looked hopeless, yet Allah delivered him each time. Even in the darkest hours he kept calling on Allah.","50. Allah judges by our last deeds. It is never too late to repent, hence Yusuf’s du‘a: “Let me die as a Muslim.”"]},{"t":"Family and parenting (8, 14, 15, 16)","l":["8. Ya‘qub was just to all his sons but loved Yusuf and Binyamin more, and the others grew jealous.","14. Yusuf went to the city’s outskirts to greet his parents and seated them on the throne. No human deserves more honour than one’s parents.","15. Allah perfected His favour on the family as on Ibrahim and Ishaq (12:6); the orphans’ treasure was saved because their father was righteous (18:82).","16. On hearing the dream, Ya‘qub did not boast; he moved to protect Yusuf, wanting the best for him in religion and this world."]},{"t":"Family under pressure (37, 45)","l":["37. Shaytan strives to divide families: he fuelled the brothers’ jealousy to the point of attempted murder. In disputes, pause, recognise his role, and seek Allah’s protection.","45. Allah can reconcile the worst of enemies and estranged relatives: He guided Yusuf to forgive and the brothers to repent."]}],"home":"Read one other story of a prophet in the Qur’an this week, as lesson 2 suggests, and share one lesson from it with your family.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Faith or family?","help":"Is each lesson about faith and Allah’s plan, or about family and parenting?","buckets":["Faith and Allah’s plan","Family and parenting"],"items":[["Firasah is a fruit of piety",0],["Tawakkul includes taking the means",0],["Ascribe good to Allah, evil to Shaytan",0],["Where you end matters most",0],["Fairness is not the same as love",1],["Honour your parents in every way",1],["Pious households produce pious children",1],["Protect your children in faith and life",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"According to lesson 3, what does Allah give someone He wants good for?","o":["Wealth","Understanding of the religion","A long life"],"a":1,"w":"Bukhari 71."},{"q":"What is firasah, according to lesson 4?","o":["A legal proof","A fruit of piety that can guide decisions","Fortune-telling"],"a":1,"w":"It carries no legal weight."},{"q":"What does lesson 8 teach parents?","o":["Love every child exactly equally","Be fair; love is beyond our control","Favour the eldest"],"a":1,"w":"Ya‘qub was just, but loved two sons more."},{"q":"How did Yusuf honour his parents (lesson 14)?","o":["He sent them money","He greeted them outside the city and seated them on the throne","He wrote to them"],"a":1,"w":"No human deserves more honour."},{"q":"What is lesson 32’s definition of tawakkul?","o":["Sitting back and waiting","Doing your best, knowing results are in Allah’s hands","Avoiding all plans"],"a":1,"w":"Yusuf asked to be mentioned to the King."},{"q":"What does lesson 50 teach?","o":["Where you begin decides everything","Where you end matters most","Lineage decides our fate"],"a":1,"w":"It is never too late to repent."}]},
{"id":30,"verses":[[5,5],[24,24],[31,31],[92,92]],"title":"Fifty Lessons II: Character and the Heart","short":"Lessons on manners, the heart’s diseases, and chastity","big":"Yusuf’s story is a portrait of noble character set against the diseases of the heart: ego, jealousy and gossip. These lessons gather what the surah teaches about manners, the dangers within us, and guarding chastity and desire.","remember":["Lesson 6: inner beauty is more attractive than outer beauty, and it grows while outer beauty fades.","Lesson 27: a believer does not hurt others’ feelings: Yusuf never asked why they threw him in the well.","Lesson 19: jealousy makes rational people irrational. Seek refuge in Allah the moment it arises.","Lesson 22: gossip distances us from Allah; leave what does not concern you, and defend the absent.","Lesson 17: guard your faith in youth and Allah will guard you in old age.","Lesson 24: desiring a sin is not a sin; controlling the urge is the essence of taqwa."],"more":[{"t":"Character and manners (6, 9, 10, 11)","l":["6. His refusal made Yusuf more attractive still. Humans naturally admire honourable character.","9. Appreciate Allah’s blessings and accept what you lack: there is good in what He gives and in what He withholds.","10. Don’t flaunt your blessings: Allah does not love the boastful, and flaunting provokes envy.","11. The believer is cautious: “not stung twice from the same hole”. Think through consequences before acting."]},{"t":"More on manners (13, 23, 27, 28, 44)","l":["13. Good strategy is part of good faith: Yusuf searched the older brothers’ bags first to avoid suspicion.","23. Haste is from Shaytan: after years in prison, Yusuf did not rush out, and was made minister.","27. Yusuf concealed his brothers’ faults and said “No blame on you today.” How many of us come close?","28. Generosity wins hearts: generous measure, returned goods, “I am the best of hosts.”","44. Be careful what you say to people you don’t trust: “I fear a wolf” became the brothers’ excuse."]},{"t":"Diseases of the heart (18, 19, 22)","l":["18. Ego calls to evil. The wife of al-‘Aziz thought she could coerce a slave, and when refused, took revenge. Care how Allah sees you, not how people do.","19. “Beware of envy, for it devours good deeds as fire devours wood” (Abu Dawud 4903).","22. “Part of the perfection of one’s Islam is leaving what does not concern him” (Ibn Majah). The tale-bearer will not enter Jannah."]},{"t":"Chastity and desire (17, 20, 21, 24)","l":["17. Yusuf resisted temptation in his most vulnerable years and was honoured later. “I’ll become pious when I’m old” rarely happens.","20. Long periods alone fuelled her lust. Islam acknowledges natural attraction and prescribes no seclusion, lowering the gaze and modest dress. We ignore this at our peril.","21. Unchecked lust blinds people and leads to sin and deceit. Among the seven under Allah’s shade is one tempted by a woman of rank and beauty who says, “I fear Allah.”","24. We are not accountable for passing thoughts we do not act on. Fear of Allah held Yusuf back."]}],"home":"Pick one disease of the heart from this lesson (ego, jealousy or gossip) and watch for it for a week. Each time it appears, say “a‘udhu billah” and do the opposite.","game":{"type":"sort","title":"Build it or beware of it?","help":"Is each a quality to build, or a disease of the heart to beware of?","buckets":["Character to build","Disease to beware of"],"items":[["Inner beauty",0],["Generosity to guests",0],["Sparing others’ feelings",0],["Caution before acting",0],["Ego",1],["Jealousy",1],["Gossip",1],["Haste",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is inner beauty more attractive, according to lesson 6?","o":["It costs nothing","It grows over time while outer beauty fades","Nobody notices outer beauty"],"a":1,"w":"Humans naturally admire honourable character."},{"q":"What does lesson 17 warn against?","o":["Delaying piety to old age","Marrying young","Travelling"],"a":0,"w":"Those who wait rarely reform."},{"q":"What is lesson 13’s example of strategy?","o":["Searching the older brothers’ bags first","Storing grain","Sending the shirt"],"a":0,"w":"So as not to draw attention."},{"q":"Which hadith supports lesson 23?","o":["Haste is from Shaytan","The believer is not stung twice","Envy eats good deeds"],"a":0,"w":"Al-Tirmidhi."},{"q":"What does lesson 44 learn from “I fear a wolf”?","o":["Wolves are dangerous","Words given to ill-intentioned people can be used against you","Always tell people your fears"],"a":1,"w":"It became the brothers’ excuse."},{"q":"According to lesson 24, what is the essence of taqwa?","o":["Never feeling any desire","Controlling an unlawful urge","Avoiding all people"],"a":1,"w":"Fear of Allah held Yusuf back."}]},
{"id":31,"verses":[[18,18],[33,33],[86,86],[110,110]],"title":"Fifty Lessons III: Patience, Repentance and Society","short":"Lessons on du‘a, tawbah, da‘wah and justice","big":"The surah’s heroes are patient, its sinners repent, and its prophet calls to Allah and serves society. These lessons gather what it teaches about patience and du‘a, repentance and forgiveness, da‘wah, justice, and life’s trials.","remember":["Lesson 12: sabrun jamil means complaining only to Allah: the pinnacle of faith in calamity.","Lesson 40: the best therapy is du‘a.","Lesson 41: Allah looks at the quality of our repentance, not the quantity of our sins.","Lesson 43: real forgiveness is forgiving at the height of power.","Lesson 29: in da‘wah, actions speak louder than words.","Lesson 38: you won’t succeed without experiencing failure."],"more":[{"t":"Patience and du‘a (12, 25, 26, 39, 40, 48)","l":["12. Three kinds of patience: in calamity, in avoiding sin, and in persevering in worship.","25 and 48. The only way to control a sinful urge is to turn to Allah: “His Lord answered him and turned their cunning away” (12:34). No disease of the heart is beyond Allah’s power to cure.","26. The fruits of patience are always sweet: from dungeon to the King’s court, rewarded without measure.","39 and 40. Complaining to Allah is part of iman; supplication in distress heals: “Prison is dearer to me…” (12:33)."]},{"t":"Repentance and forgiveness (41, 42, 43, 46, 47)","l":["41. Sinning while planning to repent is not repentance (12:9). True tawbah needs regret, remorse and the resolve never to return.","42. Sins against people need an apology to them: the brothers apologised to Yusuf and their father, then repented to Allah.","43. Yusuf forgave at the height of power, and the Prophet ﷺ repeated his words at the Ka‘bah. Neither brought up the past.","46. When confronting wrongdoers, raise their crime against Allah first: “Your souls have tempted you…” (12:18).","47. Everyone has some good: the brothers went from would-be murderers, to penitents, to prophets."]},{"t":"Da‘wah (29, 30, 31)","l":["29. Yusuf established character, honesty and ihsan first. Only after “we see you as one of the good-doers” did he preach.","30. Wait for the right setting: “Before your next meal arrives…” prepared them to listen.","31. Begin with tawhid. Islam’s creed is simple: One Allah, the prophets, and the Day of Judgement."]},{"t":"Society, law and justice (33, 34, 35)","l":["33. Civic engagement is part of Islam: Yusuf served a non-Muslim king and worked within the law of the land, without violating his faith.","34. Islam allows circumstantial evidence: the untorn bloodied shirt, and the shirt torn from the back.","35. Injustice is part of this world. Fight it by every lawful means; if justice fails here, every wronged person will get their right in Allah’s court."]},{"t":"Life’s trials (38)","l":["Born in a prophet’s household, Yusuf went through the well, slavery, false accusation and prison before the minister’s chair.","Life is a cycle of ups and downs. We won’t succeed until we show Allah we can handle failure."]}],"home":"If you have wronged someone, apologise to them this week (lesson 42), then ask Allah’s forgiveness. If someone has wronged you and apologised, forgive them without bringing up the past (lesson 43).","game":{"type":"match","title":"Lessons in a phrase","help":"Tap a term on the left, then the lesson it teaches on the right.","pairs":[["Sabrun jamil","Complaining only to Allah"],["Du‘a","The best therapy"],["Tawbah","Regret, remorse and resolve"],["“No blame on you today”","Forgiving at the height of power"],["Da‘wah","Character first, then tawhid"],["The two shirts","Circumstantial evidence"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What are the three kinds of patience (lesson 12)?","o":["In calamity, in avoiding sin, in worship","In eating, sleeping and working","In speech, silence and travel"],"a":0,"w":"Sabrun jamil is the best kind."},{"q":"What does lesson 41 say Allah looks at?","o":["The quantity of our sins","The quality of our repentance","Our lineage"],"a":1,"w":"True tawbah includes resolving never to return."},{"q":"What must you do if you wronged a person (lesson 42)?","o":["Only repent to Allah","Apologise to them, then repent to Allah","Nothing"],"a":1,"w":"Sins against people need an apology."},{"q":"What does lesson 46 advise when confronting wrongdoing?","o":["Raise the right of Allah first","Ignore Allah’s right","Only mention the victim"],"a":0,"w":"“Your souls have tempted you…”"},{"q":"What does lesson 33 draw from Yusuf’s ministry?","o":["Civic engagement can be permissible","All government work is forbidden","Muslims must rule"],"a":0,"w":"Within the limits of the faith."},{"q":"What is lesson 38?","o":["Success never involves failure","You won’t succeed without experiencing failure","Avoid all risks"],"a":1,"w":"Life is a cycle of ups and downs."}]},
/* ---------- Conclusion: The Ring Composition ---------- */
{"id":32,"verses":[43,44],"title":"The Ring Composition of Surah Yusuf","short":"The conclusion: a perfect mirror around the King’s dream","big":"Modern researchers have noticed a remarkable symmetry in Surah Yusuf. Its themes form concentric rings: the story works inwards to a central climax, the King’s dream, then outwards again in exactly the reverse order, each section mirroring its opposite. Across 111 verses, revealed in parts and recited orally, it is another sign of the Qur’an’s divine origin.","remember":["The centre (K) is the King’s dream, verses 43–44: the turning point of the story.","Each section mirrors its opposite: A with A′, B with B′, and so on.","Separation ↔ reunion, slavery ↔ authority, prison ↔ release, forgetting ↔ remembering.","Example: the sixth element (F) is Yusuf tempted by the minister’s wife; the sixth from last (F′) is Yusuf exonerated by her.","Perfect symmetry, revealed piecemeal and recited orally, shows the Qur’an’s supreme eloquence.","Our faith asks us not just to memorise knowledge, but to implement it."],"more":[{"t":"The idea","l":["The themes form concentric rings, working inwards to a centre and then outwards in reverse order.","Source: Jawad Anwar Qureshi, “Ring Composition in Surah Yusuf”, JIQSA vol. 2 (2017), slightly modified by the author."]},{"t":"The outer rings","l":["A 1–3, preface ↔ A′ 102–111, conclusion.","B 4–6, Yusuf’s dream ↔ B′ 99–101, its fulfilment.","C 7–18, Yusuf and his brothers separated from Ya‘qub ↔ C′ 58–98, reunited with Ya‘qub.","D 19–22, Yusuf a slave in Egypt ↔ D′ 54–57, keeper of the storehouses."]},{"t":"The inner rings","l":["E 23–24, Yusuf resists out of loyalty ↔ E′ 52–53, exonerated before the ministers.","F 24–29, tempted by the minister’s wife ↔ F′ 51, exonerated by her.","G 30–32, displayed before the women who cut their hands ↔ G′ 51, the women testify to his innocence.","H 33–35, imprisoned ↔ H′ 50, released.","I 36–41, interprets the prisoners’ dreams ↔ I′ 46–49, interprets the King’s dream.","J 42, the freed prisoner forgets ↔ J′ 45, he remembers."]},{"t":"The centre","l":["K 43–44: the King’s dream. Everything before builds towards it; everything after unwinds from it.","The dream that seemed to be “confused visions” to the chiefs is the hinge on which Yusuf’s whole life turns."]},{"t":"The author’s final words","l":["Read these lessons not only to admire this real-life saga, but to absorb and live them through the trials of our own lives.","This is the best of stories, and its lessons are for those who ponder.","May this be the beginning of a lifelong reflection on, and implementation of, not just this surah but the entire Qur’an. Amin."]}],"home":"Read Surah Yusuf from start to finish this month, in Arabic or translation. As you read, mark each pair of mirrored sections, and write down one lesson you will live by.","game":{"type":"match","title":"Mirror pairs","help":"Tap a section on the left, then its mirror on the right.","pairs":[["Yusuf’s dream (B)","The dream fulfilled (B′)"],["Separated from Ya‘qub (C)","Reunited with Ya‘qub (C′)"],["A slave in Egypt (D)","Keeper of the storehouses (D′)"],["Imprisoned (H)","Released from prison (H′)"],["Interprets the prisoners’ dreams (I)","Interprets the King’s dream (I′)"],["The freed prisoner forgets (J)","The freed prisoner remembers (J′)"]]},"quiz":[{"q":"What is at the centre of the ring composition?","o":["The well","The King’s dream","The reunion"],"a":1,"w":"Verses 43–44, the turning point."},{"q":"What does the preface (verses 1–3) mirror?","o":["The King’s dream","The conclusion (102–111)","The prison"],"a":1,"w":"A and A′."},{"q":"What mirrors “Yusuf imprisoned” (H)?","o":["Yusuf released from prison (H′)","The well","The banquet"],"a":0,"w":"Imprisonment ↔ release."},{"q":"What is the mirror of “Yusuf tempted by the minister’s wife” (F)?","o":["The women cut their hands","Yusuf exonerated by her (F′)","The cup in the bag"],"a":1,"w":"The sixth element and the sixth from last."},{"q":"Why is the ring composition remarkable?","o":["The surah was written in one sitting","It was revealed in parts and recited orally, yet is perfectly symmetrical","It is the longest surah"],"a":1,"w":"Another sign of its divine origin."},{"q":"What does the author ask of us in his final words?","o":["Only to memorise","To implement the lessons in our lives","To read other books"],"a":1,"w":"Absorb and live them."}]},
];

